# PDF documents written and read. Writing makes pages of any size and draws
# on them: text in the 14 standard fonts, measured by Adobe's own metrics, or
# in TrueType fonts embedded, cut down to the glyphs drawn and measured by
# their own tables, wrapped into boxes and laid out as simple tables; lines, rectangles,
# circles and paths; colours, opacity, clipping and transforms; JPEG images
# passed through, PNG and images in memory embedded with their alpha;
# links, bookmarks and the document's information. Reading takes a file's
# structure (cross-reference tables and streams, object streams,
# incremental updates, a damaged table rebuilt), its pages, its
# information, its bookmarks and links, any object, and the text of a page,
# decoded through the standard encodings, a font's differences and its
# ToUnicode map. Written from ISO 32000-1:2008 (PDF 1.7); what it writes
# Poppler's `pdftotext` and `pdfinfo`, Ghostscript and pypdf read without a
# complaint, and the text it reads is the text `pdftotext` reads.
#
# Text in a standard font is what those fonts show: the characters of
# WinAnsiEncoding (Latin-1 and the euro, the dashes and the quotation marks)
# and those of Symbol and ZapfDingbats; any other character needs a font
# embedded, which `load_font` reads from the bytes of a TrueType file and
# which every glyph it draws is written from, two bytes a code, with the map
# that lets a reader copy the text out. It embeds no font with CFF outlines
# and no Type 1 font, shapes no script (a ligature, a mark placed, a
# right-to-left run) and kerns no pair: a text is its characters' glyphs in a
# row. It decodes no image
# itself: PNG is decoded by `image/png`, and a page's images are read as
# their bytes. It opens no path (`fs` does) and draws no chart. It edits no
# file it read and writes no encrypted file. A file encrypted with RC4 by
# the standard security handler is read with its password; one encrypted
# with AES is refused `unsupported`.
#
# **A file from outside is not trusted.** Reading takes `Limits`: the
# objects a file may hold, the bytes a stream may decode to, how deep its
# values may nest, its pages and its revisions; a file past one fails
# `limit` before the memory is spent. Nothing a file holds makes a call
# abort, loop without end or read past the file.
#
# It fails with `invalid`, `limit`, `not_found` and `unsupported`, and with
# the codes of `compress` and `image/png` where a stream or an image does not
# decode.
#
# Not written yet, each a `pending` block of its ledger: OpenType fonts with
# CFF outlines embedded; spot colours and ICC colour spaces; blend modes,
# overprint and soft masks of the graphics state; XMP metadata streams;
# reusable form objects (templates); optional content: layers shown or hidden;
# linear and radial gradients, shadings; files attached to a document; text
# notes, highlights, free text, ink, and other annotations; interactive forms:
# fields read, filled, flattened; how a viewer opens the document: page mode,
# layout, preferences, labels, open action; documents written encrypted, with
# passwords and permissions; the text of a composite font that gives no
# ToUnicode map, read from the embedded font's own cmap table, and of the
# predefined CJK CMaps; files encrypted with AES; the images a page draws,
# their bytes; the fonts a file uses, listed and extracted; a file read
# changed and written again: pages merged, split, inserted, removed, rotated,
# cropped; objects changed, renumbered, pruned; incremental updates written;
# digital signatures and certificates.

use bytes
use image
use image/png
use math
use pdf/internal/assemble
use pdf/internal/compose
use pdf/internal/dates
use pdf/internal/embedded
use pdf/internal/filters
use pdf/internal/font_samples
use pdf/internal/images
use pdf/internal/measure
use pdf/internal/model
use pdf/internal/navigation
use pdf/internal/samples
use pdf/internal/store
use pdf/internal/syntax
use pdf/internal/tables
use pdf/internal/text as page_text
use pdf/internal/text_strings
use pdf/internal/wrap
use time

## Codes

# The code of a file that is not what PDF says, the message naming the
# byte; of a value a writer is given that PDF has no form for; and of an
# object asked for as a kind it is not. `decode`, `text_of`, `pieces_of`,
# `parse_date`, `parse_color`, `add_jpeg`, `add_png`, `encode` and the
# readers of objects fail with it.
constant INVALID: str
    "invalid"

# The code of a file that would pass the caller's `Limits`. `decode`,
# `text_of`, `pieces_of` and `stream_data_of` fail with it.
constant LIMIT: str
    "limit"

# The code of a key a dictionary does not hold (`entry_of`) and of a font
# name that is none of the 14 (`parse_font`).
constant NOT_FOUND: str
    "not_found"

# The code of what this package does not do: a character a font has no glyph
# for (`width_of`, `draw_text`), a font it does not embed (`load_font`: CFF
# outlines, a licence that bars embedding), an image it does not embed
# (`add_png`), a file encrypted with AES (`decode`), a stream of an image
# filter (`stream_data_of`).
constant UNSUPPORTED: str
    "unsupported"

## Sizes and units

# A width and a height, in points: 72 to the inch, as PDF measures.
record Size
    # The width in points, more than 0.
    width: f64
    # The height in points, more than 0.
    height: f64

# The usual sizes of paper.
variant Paper
    # ISO A0, 841 by 1189 mm.
    a0
    # ISO A1, 594 by 841 mm.
    a1
    # ISO A2, 420 by 594 mm.
    a2
    # ISO A3, 297 by 420 mm.
    a3
    # ISO A4, 210 by 297 mm.
    a4
    # ISO A5, 148 by 210 mm.
    a5
    # ISO A6, 105 by 148 mm.
    a6
    # ISO B4, 250 by 353 mm.
    b4
    # ISO B5, 176 by 250 mm.
    b5
    # US Letter, 8.5 by 11 inches.
    letter
    # US Legal, 8.5 by 14 inches.
    legal
    # Tabloid, 11 by 17 inches.
    tabloid
    # Executive, 7.25 by 10.5 inches.
    executive

# What `to_points` converts from.
variant Unit
    # A point, 1/72 of an inch: PDF's own unit.
    point
    # An inch, 72 points.
    inch
    # A millimetre, 72/25.4 points.
    mm
    # A centimetre, 720/25.4 points.
    cm
    # A pica, 12 points.
    pica

# The size of `paper`, upright, in points: the millimetres converted
# exactly, so A4 is 595.2756 by 841.8898.
#
# - `paper`: the paper asked about.
#
# See also `to_landscape` and `to_points`.
function size_of(paper: Paper) -> Size
    return match paper
        .a0        => Size(width: to_points(841.0, unit: .mm), height: to_points(1189.0, unit: .mm))
        .a1        => Size(width: to_points(594.0, unit: .mm), height: to_points(841.0, unit: .mm))
        .a2        => Size(width: to_points(420.0, unit: .mm), height: to_points(594.0, unit: .mm))
        .a3        => Size(width: to_points(297.0, unit: .mm), height: to_points(420.0, unit: .mm))
        .a4        => Size(width: to_points(210.0, unit: .mm), height: to_points(297.0, unit: .mm))
        .a5        => Size(width: to_points(148.0, unit: .mm), height: to_points(210.0, unit: .mm))
        .a6        => Size(width: to_points(105.0, unit: .mm), height: to_points(148.0, unit: .mm))
        .b4        => Size(width: to_points(250.0, unit: .mm), height: to_points(353.0, unit: .mm))
        .b5        => Size(width: to_points(176.0, unit: .mm), height: to_points(250.0, unit: .mm))
        .letter    => Size(width: 612.0, height: 792.0)
        .legal     => Size(width: 612.0, height: 1008.0)
        .tabloid   => Size(width: 792.0, height: 1224.0)
        .executive => Size(width: 522.0, height: 756.0)

# `size` turned to lie on its long side: the wider of its two lengths as
# the width.
#
# - `size`: any size; a square comes back as it was.
#
# See also `to_portrait`.
function to_landscape(size: Size) -> Size
    if size.width >= size.height
        return size
    return Size(width: size.height, height: size.width)

# `size` turned to stand on its short side: the taller of its two lengths
# as the height.
#
# - `size`: any size; a square comes back as it was.
#
# See also `to_landscape`.
function to_portrait(size: Size) -> Size
    if size.height >= size.width
        return size
    return Size(width: size.height, height: size.width)

# `n` of `unit` in points.
#
# - `n`: the length; any value, negative ones included.
# - `unit`: what `n` counts.
#
# See also `size_of`.
function to_points(n: f64, unit: Unit) -> f64
    return match unit
        .point => n
        .inch  => n * 72.0
        .mm    => n * 72.0 / 25.4
        .cm    => n * 720.0 / 25.4
        .pica  => n * 12.0

## Colours

# A colour, each component from 0 to 1; one outside is written as the
# nearest end.
variant Color
    # A shade of gray.
    gray
        # 0 black to 1 white.
        level: f64
    # A colour of light, as screens make it.
    rgb
        # Red, 0 to 1.
        red: f64
        # Green, 0 to 1.
        green: f64
        # Blue, 0 to 1.
        blue: f64
    # A colour of ink, as printers make it.
    cmyk
        # Cyan, 0 to 1.
        cyan: f64
        # Magenta, 0 to 1.
        magenta: f64
        # Yellow, 0 to 1.
        yellow: f64
        # Black, 0 to 1.
        black: f64

# The colour `s` writes in CSS's hexadecimal form: `#rrggbb` or `#rgb`, in
# either case.
#
# - `s`: the text, its `#` included.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `s` is not one of the two forms.
#
# See also `Color`.
function parse_color(s: str) -> Color?
    if s.len() == 0 || s[0] != '#'
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"\"{s}\" is not a colour: it is written #rrggbb or #rgb")
    digits: [str] @= []

    for c in s.slice(from: 1, to: s.len()).chars()
        digits @ digits.push(c)

        if s.len() == 4
            digits @ digits.push(c)

    raw = match bytes.parse_hex(digits.join(""))
        .ok r  => r
        .err _ => return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"\"{s}\" is not a colour: it is written #rrggbb or #rgb")

    if raw.len() != 3
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"\"{s}\" is not a colour: it is written #rrggbb or #rgb")
    return ok(.rgb(red: to_f64(raw[0]) / 255.0, green: to_f64(raw[1]) / 255.0, blue: to_f64(raw[2]) / 255.0))

## Fonts

# A font text is drawn in: one of the 14 standard fonts, which every reader of
# PDF has and no file embeds, or a TrueType font of the program's own, which
# the file carries, cut down to the glyphs drawn.
# The Latin twelve of the standard ones show WinAnsiEncoding's characters;
# Symbol shows Greek letters and mathematical signs, ZapfDingbats ornaments;
# an embedded font shows every character it has a glyph for.
variant Font
    # Helvetica.
    helvetica
    # Helvetica, bold.
    helvetica_bold
    # Helvetica, slanted.
    helvetica_oblique
    # Helvetica, bold and slanted.
    helvetica_bold_oblique
    # Times, upright.
    times_roman
    # Times, bold.
    times_bold
    # Times, italic.
    times_italic
    # Times, bold and italic.
    times_bold_italic
    # Courier, every character 600 thousandths wide.
    courier
    # Courier, bold.
    courier_bold
    # Courier, slanted.
    courier_oblique
    # Courier, bold and slanted.
    courier_bold_oblique
    # Symbol: Greek letters and mathematical signs.
    symbol
    # ZapfDingbats: arrows, stars, check marks and other ornaments.
    zapf_dingbats
    # A TrueType font embedded in the file, made by `load_font`.
    embedded
        # The font, read.
        face: embedded.Face

# What an AFM file says of a font as a whole, in thousandths of the font's
# size: multiply by the size and divide by 1000 for points.
record FontMetrics
    # How high a letter with an ascender reaches above the baseline.
    ascent: i64
    # How far a descender reaches below the baseline: negative.
    descent: i64
    # The height of a capital; 0 for Symbol and ZapfDingbats, whose files
    # name none.
    cap_height: i64
    # The height of a small letter without an ascender; 0 for Symbol and
    # ZapfDingbats.
    x_height: i64
    # The slant of upright strokes in degrees, negative leaning right.
    italic_angle: f64
    # Where an underline stands: negative, below the baseline.
    underline_position: i64
    # How thick an underline is.
    underline_thickness: i64
    # The left of the box every glyph fits in.
    box_left: i64
    # The bottom of that box.
    box_bottom: i64
    # The right of that box.
    box_right: i64
    # The top of that box.
    box_top: i64

# The name PDF gives `font`, as its `/BaseFont` writes it: `Helvetica-Bold`.
# An embedded font's is its PostScript name (its full name, else `EmbeddedFont`,
# where it has none), cut to letters, digits, `-`, `_` and `.`; the file writes
# it after a six-letter tag, `AAAAAA+NotoSans-Regular`.
#
# - `font`: the font.
#
# See also `parse_font`.
function base_font_of(font: Font) -> str
    return match font
        .helvetica              => "Helvetica"
        .helvetica_bold         => "Helvetica-Bold"
        .helvetica_oblique      => "Helvetica-Oblique"
        .helvetica_bold_oblique => "Helvetica-BoldOblique"
        .times_roman            => "Times-Roman"
        .times_bold             => "Times-Bold"
        .times_italic           => "Times-Italic"
        .times_bold_italic      => "Times-BoldItalic"
        .courier                => "Courier"
        .courier_bold           => "Courier-Bold"
        .courier_oblique        => "Courier-Oblique"
        .courier_bold_oblique   => "Courier-BoldOblique"
        .symbol                 => "Symbol"
        .zapf_dingbats          => "ZapfDingbats"
        .embedded e             => e.face.name

# The standard font PDF names `name`: `Times-Roman` is `times_roman`.
#
# - `name`: the name exactly as `base_font_of` writes it.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `name` is none of the 14.
#
# See also `base_font_of`.
function parse_font(name: str) -> Font?
    return match name
        "Helvetica"             => ok(.helvetica)
        "Helvetica-Bold"        => ok(.helvetica_bold)
        "Helvetica-Oblique"     => ok(.helvetica_oblique)
        "Helvetica-BoldOblique" => ok(.helvetica_bold_oblique)
        "Times-Roman"           => ok(.times_roman)
        "Times-Bold"            => ok(.times_bold)
        "Times-Italic"          => ok(.times_italic)
        "Times-BoldItalic"      => ok(.times_bold_italic)
        "Courier"               => ok(.courier)
        "Courier-Bold"          => ok(.courier_bold)
        "Courier-Oblique"       => ok(.courier_oblique)
        "Courier-BoldOblique"   => ok(.courier_bold_oblique)
        "Symbol"                => ok(.symbol)
        "ZapfDingbats"          => ok(.zapf_dingbats)
        _                       => fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"\"{name}\" is none of the 14 standard fonts")

# What the writer holds of `font` to measure with: its number, 0 to 13 for a
# standard font and 14 for an embedded one, and the slots that number is read
# against.
record Measured
    # The font's number.
    number: i64
    # The embedded fonts that number names: one, or none.
    slots: [embedded.Slot]

# `font` as the writer measures it: the number it has and the slots that number
# is read against, so that a font that is not a document's yet can be measured.
#
# - `font`: the font.
#
# See also `font_number`.
function measured(font: Font) -> Measured
    return match font
        .embedded e => Measured(number: 14, slots: [embedded.Slot(face: e.face, used: {})])
        .helvetica | .helvetica_bold | .helvetica_oblique | .helvetica_bold_oblique | .times_roman | .times_bold | .times_italic | .times_bold_italic | .courier | .courier_bold | .courier_oblique | .courier_bold_oblique | .symbol | .zapf_dingbats => Measured(
            number: measure.font_index_of(base_font_of(font)),
            slots: []
        )

# The number the pen gives `font` in `doc`: an embedded font is added to the
# document where it is not there already.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `font`: the font.
#
# See also `measured`.
function font_number(@doc: Document, font: Font) -> i64
    match font
        .embedded e => return compose.number_of(@doc.state, face: e.face)
        .helvetica | .helvetica_bold | .helvetica_oblique | .helvetica_bold_oblique | .times_roman | .times_bold | .times_italic | .times_bold_italic | .courier | .courier_bold | .courier_oblique | .courier_bold_oblique | .symbol | .zapf_dingbats => return measure.font_index_of(
            base_font_of(font)
        )

# A TrueType font made ready to draw with: read from the bytes of its file, and
# carried by every document that draws with it, cut down to the glyphs drawn,
# with a `ToUnicode` map so that a reader copies the text out. The font file
# is not kept open or read again: `data` is held.
#
# - `data`: the bytes of a `.ttf` or `.ttc` file, or an `.otf` with TrueType
#   outlines.
# - `index`: which font of a collection, from 0; 0 for a single font.
#
# Fails with the codes of `font` where `data` is no font (`invalid`,
# `not_found`), and `unsupported` where the font has CFF outlines, has no
# `cmap` that is read, or sets the bits of its `OS/2` licence that bar
# embedding it or cutting it down, the message naming which.
#
# See also `set_font`, `width_of` and `draw_text`.
function load_font(data: [u8], index: i64) -> Font?
    return ok(.embedded(face: embedded.load(data, index: index)?))

# The metrics of `font`, from Adobe's AFM file of it, or from the font itself
# where it is embedded: its `hhea`, `OS/2`, `post` and `head` tables scaled to
# thousandths of the size. An embedded font's `cap_height` is `OS/2`'s, else the
# height of its `H`, else 0, and its stem is not a metric of the font but an
# estimate from its weight.
#
# - `font`: the font.
#
# See also `width_of`.
function metrics_of(font: Font) -> FontMetrics
    m = measured(font)
    g = measure.global_of(m.number, slots: m.slots)
    return FontMetrics(
        ascent: g.ascent,
        descent: g.descent,
        cap_height: g.cap_height,
        x_height: g.x_height,
        italic_angle: g.italic_angle,
        underline_position: g.underline_position,
        underline_thickness: g.underline_thickness,
        box_left: g.box[0],
        box_bottom: g.box[1],
        box_right: g.box[2],
        box_top: g.box[3]
    )

# How wide `text` is in `font` at `size`, in points: the sum of its
# characters' widths in Adobe's AFM file, or in the embedded font's own `hmtx`
# table, rounded as the file's width array writes them, with no kerning, as PDF
# shows it.
#
# - `text`: the text; the empty text is 0 wide. A line break counts as no
#   character.
# - `font`: the font.
# - `size`: the size in points, 0 or more.
#
# Fails `unsupported` where `font` has no glyph for a character of `text`,
# the message naming it.
#
# See also `has_glyphs` and `wrap_text`.
function width_of(text: str, font: Font, size: f64) -> f64?
    m = measured(font)
    shown = measure.codes_of(m.number, slots: m.slots, text: text)?
    return ok(measure.width_of_codes(m.number, slots: m.slots, shown: shown, size: size, spacing: measure.plain()))

# Whether `font` has a glyph for every character of `text`, so that
# `draw_text` can show it.
#
# - `font`: the font.
# - `text`: the text; the empty text is shown by every font.
#
# See also `width_of`.
function has_glyphs(font: Font, text: str) -> bool
    m = measured(font)
    return measure.shows_all(m.number, slots: m.slots, text: text)

## Documents

# A document being written: its pages and what is drawn on them, the pen
# that draws, its images, links, bookmarks and information. Made by
# `document`; `encode` writes it as a file.
record Document
    # Everything written so far.
    state: compose.Doc

# A document of no page, its content compressed, its pen Helvetica at 12
# points in black, lines 1 point wide.
#
# See also `add_page` and `encode`.
function document() -> Document
    return Document(state: compose.start())

# Sets an entry of the document's information: `Title`, `Author`,
# `Subject`, `Keywords`, `Creator`, `Producer`, `CreationDate`, `ModDate`,
# or a key of the program's own.
#
# - `doc`: the document, whose entry is set or replaced.
# - `key`: the entry's key, without a slash; not empty.
# - `value`: its text, any character; a date as `format_date` writes it.
#
# Aborts where `key` is empty.
#
# See also `format_date` and `info_of`.
function set_info(@doc: Document, key: str, value: str)
    assert key != ""
    doc.state.info[key] @ value

# `t` as PDF writes a date, in UTC: `D:20261118103000Z`.
#
# - `t`: any instant; its fraction of a second is dropped.
#
# See also `parse_date` and `set_info`.
function format_date(t: time.Instant) -> str
    dt = time.to_date_time(t)
    return dates.format(
        year: dt.year,
        month: dt.month,
        day: dt.day,
        hour: dt.hour,
        minute: dt.minute,
        second: dt.second
    )

# Sets whether the document's page content is compressed, which it is
# unless asked otherwise.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `on`: `true` to compress with DEFLATE, `false` to write it as text.
#
# See also `encode`.
function set_compression(@doc: Document, on: bool)
    doc.state.compress @ on

# Adds a page of `size` after the last one and draws on it from now on,
# with the pen as it stands.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `size`: the page's size; each length from 1 to 14,400 points, PDF's
#   own bounds.
#
# Aborts where a length of `size` is outside 1 to 14,400.
#
# See also `size_of` and `set_page`.
function add_page(@doc: Document, size: Size)
    assert size.width >= 1.0 && size.width <= 14400.0 && size.height >= 1.0 && size.height <= 14400.0
    compose.add_page(@doc.state, width: size.width, height: size.height)

# How many pages `doc` has.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
#
# See also `add_page`.
function page_count(doc: Document) -> i64
    return doc.state.pages.len()

# Draws on the page at `index` from now on, as on a page just added: what
# is drawn goes on top of what it holds.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `index`: the page, from 0 to `page_count(doc) - 1`.
#
# Aborts where `index` is outside the pages.
#
# See also `page_count`.
function set_page(@doc: Document, index: i64)
    assert index >= 0 && index < doc.state.pages.len()
    compose.set_page(@doc.state, index: index)

## The pen

# How the ends of a stroked line are drawn.
variant LineCap
    # Square, at the end itself.
    butt
    # A half circle past the end.
    round
    # A half square past the end.
    square

# How two strokes meet at a corner.
variant LineJoin
    # A sharp corner, cut where it passes the miter limit.
    miter
    # A rounded corner.
    round
    # A corner cut flat.
    bevel

# How the characters of text are painted.
variant TextMode
    # Filled with the fill colour: the usual.
    fill
    # Outlined with the stroke colour.
    stroke
    # Filled, then outlined.
    fill_and_stroke
    # Not painted: the text is there to be searched and copied.
    invisible

# The text state beside the font. `text_style` makes the usual one.
record TextStyle
    # Points added after every character, 0 or more.
    character_spacing: f64
    # Points added after every space, 0 or more.
    word_spacing: f64
    # The width of every character in percent of its own, 100 for none.
    horizontal_scale: f64
    # Points the baseline is raised, negative to lower it.
    rise: f64
    # How the characters are painted.
    mode: TextMode

# A transformation of the plane, as PDF's six numbers: a point (x, y) goes
# to (a x + c y + e, b x + d y + f). `identity`, `translation`, `rotation`
# and `scaling` make the usual ones, and `multiply` combines them.
record Matrix
    # The x of the image of (1, 0), less `e`.
    a: f64
    # The y of the image of (1, 0), less `f`.
    b: f64
    # The x of the image of (0, 1), less `e`.
    c: f64
    # The y of the image of (0, 1), less `f`.
    d: f64
    # How far the plane moves right.
    e: f64
    # How far the plane moves up.
    f: f64

# Sets the font text is drawn and measured in from now on.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `font`: the font.
# - `size`: the size in points, more than 0.
#
# Aborts where `size` is not more than 0.
#
# See also `draw_text` and `set_text_style`.
function set_font(@doc: Document, font: Font, size: f64)
    assert size > 0.0
    doc.state.pen.font @ font_number(@doc, font: font)
    doc.state.pen.size @ size

# Sets the colour shapes are filled with and text is drawn in.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `color`: the colour.
#
# See also `set_stroke_color` and `set_opacity`.
function set_fill_color(@doc: Document, color: Color)
    ink = match color
        .gray g => compose.Ink(space: 0, parts: [g.level])
        .rgb c  => compose.Ink(space: 1, parts: [c.red, c.green, c.blue])
        .cmyk c => compose.Ink(space: 2, parts: [c.cyan, c.magenta, c.yellow, c.black])

    compose.set_fill(@doc.state, ink: ink)

# Sets the colour lines and outlines are drawn in.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `color`: the colour.
#
# See also `set_fill_color`.
function set_stroke_color(@doc: Document, color: Color)
    ink = match color
        .gray g => compose.Ink(space: 0, parts: [g.level])
        .rgb c  => compose.Ink(space: 1, parts: [c.red, c.green, c.blue])
        .cmyk c => compose.Ink(space: 2, parts: [c.cyan, c.magenta, c.yellow, c.black])

    compose.set_stroke(@doc.state, ink: ink)

# Sets how wide lines are drawn.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `width`: the width in points, 0 or more; 0 is the thinnest line the
#   device can draw.
#
# Aborts where `width` is negative.
#
# See also `set_dash`.
function set_line_width(@doc: Document, width: f64)
    assert width >= 0.0
    doc.state.pen.line_width @ width
    compose.put_if_page(@doc.state, op: syntax.real(width) + " w")

# Sets how the ends of lines are drawn.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `style`: the cap.
#
# See also `set_line_join`.
function set_line_cap(@doc: Document, style: LineCap)
    cap = match style
        .butt   => 0
        .round  => 1
        .square => 2

    doc.state.pen.cap @ cap
    compose.put_if_page(@doc.state, op: f"{cap} J")

# Sets how lines meet at a corner.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `style`: the join.
#
# See also `set_miter_limit`.
function set_line_join(@doc: Document, style: LineJoin)
    join_style = match style
        .miter => 0
        .round => 1
        .bevel => 2

    doc.state.pen.join @ join_style
    compose.put_if_page(@doc.state, op: f"{join_style} j")

# Sets how far a mitered corner may reach before it is cut, as a ratio of
# its length to the line's width.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `limit`: 1 or more; 10 is PDF's own.
#
# Aborts where `limit` is below 1.
#
# See also `set_line_join`.
function set_miter_limit(@doc: Document, limit: f64)
    assert limit >= 1.0
    doc.state.pen.miter @ limit
    compose.put_if_page(@doc.state, op: syntax.real(limit) + " M")

# Sets the dashes lines are drawn with.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `lengths`: the lengths of dash and gap, alternately, in points, each 0
#   or more and not all 0; none for a solid line.
# - `phase`: how far into the pattern a line begins, in points.
#
# Aborts where a length is negative or every length is 0.
#
# See also `set_line_width`.
function set_dash(@doc: Document, lengths: [f64], phase: f64)
    some @= false

    for length in lengths
        assert length >= 0.0

        if length > 0.0
            some @ true

    assert lengths.len() == 0 || some
    doc.state.pen.dash @ lengths
    doc.state.pen.phase @ phase
    compose.put_if_page(@doc.state, op: compose.dash_op(doc.state.pen))

# Sets how opaque fills, text and strokes are drawn.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `fill`: 0 transparent to 1 opaque, for fills and text.
# - `stroke`: the same, for lines.
#
# Aborts where either is outside 0 to 1.
#
# See also `set_fill_color`.
function set_opacity(@doc: Document, fill: f64, stroke: f64)
    assert fill >= 0.0 && fill <= 1.0 && stroke >= 0.0 && stroke <= 1.0
    doc.state.pen.fill_opacity @ fill
    doc.state.pen.stroke_opacity @ stroke

    if doc.state.at >= 0
        op = compose.opacity_op(@doc.state)
        compose.put(@doc.state, op: op)

# The usual text state: no spacing added, a scale of 100, no rise, the
# characters filled.
#
# See also `set_text_style`.
function text_style() -> TextStyle
    return TextStyle(character_spacing: 0.0, word_spacing: 0.0, horizontal_scale: 100.0, rise: 0.0, mode: .fill)

# Sets the text state text is drawn and measured with from now on.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `style`: the state; a horizontal scale of more than 0.
#
# Aborts where the horizontal scale is not more than 0.
#
# See also `text_style` and `draw_text`.
function set_text_style(@doc: Document, style: TextStyle)
    assert style.horizontal_scale > 0.0

    mode = match style.mode
        .fill            => 0
        .stroke          => 1
        .fill_and_stroke => 2
        .invisible       => 3

    s = compose.Style(
        character_spacing: style.character_spacing,
        word_spacing: style.word_spacing,
        horizontal_scale: style.horizontal_scale,
        rise: style.rise,
        mode: mode
    )
    doc.state.pen.style @ s
    compose.put_if_page(@doc.state, op: compose.style_ops(s))

# Saves the pen, the transform and the clipping on a stack, for
# `restore_state` to bring back.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `restore_state`, `transform` and `clip`.
function save_state(@doc: Document)
    compose.save(@doc.state)

# Brings back the pen, the transform and the clipping the last
# `save_state` on this page saved.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
#
# Aborts where this page holds no state saved and not restored.
#
# See also `save_state`.
function restore_state(@doc: Document)
    compose.restore(@doc.state)

# The transform that changes nothing.
#
# See also `multiply`.
function identity() -> Matrix
    return Matrix(a: 1.0, b: 0.0, c: 0.0, d: 1.0, e: 0.0, f: 0.0)

# The transform that moves the plane `x` right and `y` up.
#
# - `x`: the distance right, in points; negative moves left.
# - `y`: the distance up, in points; negative moves down.
#
# See also `transform`.
function translation(x: f64, y: f64) -> Matrix
    return Matrix(a: 1.0, b: 0.0, c: 0.0, d: 1.0, e: x, f: y)

# The transform that turns the plane about its origin.
#
# - `degrees`: the angle, counterclockwise; negative turns clockwise.
#
# See also `transform`.
function rotation(degrees: f64) -> Matrix
    radians = degrees * math.PI / 180.0
    cos = math.cos(radians)
    sin = math.sin(radians)
    return Matrix(a: cos, b: sin, c: -sin, d: cos, e: 0.0, f: 0.0)

# The transform that stretches the plane from its origin.
#
# - `x`: the factor across; negative mirrors.
# - `y`: the factor up; negative mirrors.
#
# See also `transform`.
function scaling(x: f64, y: f64) -> Matrix
    return Matrix(a: x, b: 0.0, c: 0.0, d: y, e: 0.0, f: 0.0)

# The transform that does `first`, then `then`.
#
# - `first`: the transform done first.
# - `then`: the transform done after it.
#
# See also `transform`.
function multiply(first: Matrix, then: Matrix) -> Matrix
    return Matrix(
        a: first.a * then.a + first.b * then.c,
        b: first.a * then.b + first.b * then.d,
        c: first.c * then.a + first.d * then.c,
        d: first.c * then.b + first.d * then.d,
        e: first.e * then.a + first.f * then.c + then.e,
        f: first.e * then.b + first.f * then.d + then.f
    )

# Transforms the coordinates of everything drawn from now on by `matrix`,
# before the transform in force takes them to the page; `save_state` and
# `restore_state` bound it.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `matrix`: the transform.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `translation`, `rotation`, `scaling` and `save_state`.
function transform(@doc: Document, matrix: Matrix)
    numbers = syntax.reals([matrix.a, matrix.b, matrix.c, matrix.d, matrix.e, matrix.f])
    compose.put(@doc.state, op: numbers.slice(from: 1, to: numbers.len() - 1) + " cm")

## Lines and shapes

# How a shape is painted.
variant Paint
    # Its outline drawn with the stroke colour.
    stroke
    # Its inside filled with the fill colour.
    fill
    # Filled, then outlined.
    fill_and_stroke

# A point of the page, in points from its bottom left corner.
record Point
    # The distance from the left edge.
    x: f64
    # The distance from the bottom edge.
    y: f64

# A path: lines and curves drawn by `draw_path` or clipped to by `clip`.
# Made by `empty_path` and built by `move_to`, `line_to`, `curve_to` and
# `close_path`.
record Path
    # The path's operators, as the content stream writes them.
    steps: [str]

# Draws a straight line from one point to another, with the stroke colour.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `from_x`: the start's distance from the left, in points.
# - `from_y`: the start's distance from the bottom.
# - `to_x`: the end's distance from the left.
# - `to_y`: the end's distance from the bottom.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `draw_polyline` and `draw_path`.
function draw_line(@doc: Document, from_x: f64, from_y: f64, to_x: f64, to_y: f64)
    r = syntax.real
    compose.put(@doc.state, op: f"{r(from_x)} {r(from_y)} m {r(to_x)} {r(to_y)} l S")

# Draws a rectangle whose bottom left corner is at (`x`, `y`).
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `x`: the left side, in points from the page's left.
# - `y`: the bottom side, in points from the page's bottom.
# - `width`: the width; negative draws to the left of `x`.
# - `height`: the height; negative draws below `y`.
# - `paint`: how it is painted.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `draw_rounded_rect`.
function draw_rect(@doc: Document, x: f64, y: f64, width: f64, height: f64, paint: Paint)
    paint_code = match paint
        .stroke          => 0
        .fill            => 1
        .fill_and_stroke => 2

    r = syntax.real
    compose.put(@doc.state, op: f"{r(x)} {r(y)} {r(width)} {r(height)} re {compose.paint_op(paint_code)}")

# Draws a rectangle with corners rounded to `radius`.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `x`: the left side.
# - `y`: the bottom side.
# - `width`: the width, more than 0.
# - `height`: the height, more than 0.
# - `radius`: the corners' radius, 0 or more; one past half the shorter
#   side is drawn as that half.
# - `paint`: how it is painted.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added, or where `width` or `height` is
# not more than 0, or `radius` is negative.
#
# See also `draw_rect`.
function draw_rounded_rect(@doc: Document, x: f64, y: f64, width: f64, height: f64, radius: f64, paint: Paint)
    paint_code = match paint
        .stroke          => 0
        .fill            => 1
        .fill_and_stroke => 2

    assert width > 0.0 && height > 0.0 && radius >= 0.0
    compose.put(
        @doc.state,
        op: compose.rounded_steps(x: x, y: y, width: width, height: height, radius: radius) + " " + compose.paint_op(paint_code)
    )

# Draws a circle, as four Bézier curves.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `center_x`: the centre's distance from the left.
# - `center_y`: the centre's distance from the bottom.
# - `radius`: the radius, 0 or more.
# - `paint`: how it is painted.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added, or where `radius` is negative.
#
# See also `draw_ellipse`.
function draw_circle(@doc: Document, center_x: f64, center_y: f64, radius: f64, paint: Paint)
    paint_code = match paint
        .stroke          => 0
        .fill            => 1
        .fill_and_stroke => 2

    assert radius >= 0.0
    compose.put(
        @doc.state,
        op: compose.ellipse_steps(cx: center_x, cy: center_y, rx: radius, ry: radius) + " " + compose.paint_op(paint_code)
    )

# Draws an ellipse whose axes are level and upright, as four Bézier curves.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `center_x`: the centre's distance from the left.
# - `center_y`: the centre's distance from the bottom.
# - `radius_x`: half its width, 0 or more.
# - `radius_y`: half its height, 0 or more.
# - `paint`: how it is painted.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added, or where a radius is negative.
#
# See also `draw_circle`.
function draw_ellipse(@doc: Document, center_x: f64, center_y: f64, radius_x: f64, radius_y: f64, paint: Paint)
    paint_code = match paint
        .stroke          => 0
        .fill            => 1
        .fill_and_stroke => 2

    assert radius_x >= 0.0 && radius_y >= 0.0
    compose.put(
        @doc.state,
        op: compose.ellipse_steps(cx: center_x, cy: center_y, rx: radius_x, ry: radius_y) + " " + compose.paint_op(paint_code)
    )

# Draws the closed shape whose corners are `points`, in order.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `points`: the corners; fewer than two draw nothing.
# - `paint`: how it is painted.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `draw_polyline`.
function draw_polygon(@doc: Document, points: [Point], paint: Paint)
    paint_code = match paint
        .stroke          => 0
        .fill            => 1
        .fill_and_stroke => 2

    if points.len() < 2
        return
    xs: [f64] @= []
    ys: [f64] @= []

    for p in points
        xs @ xs.push(p.x)
        ys @ ys.push(p.y)

    compose.put(@doc.state, op: compose.poly_steps(xs: xs, ys: ys, closed: true) + " " + compose.paint_op(paint_code))

# Draws the line through `points`, in order, not closed, with the stroke
# colour.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `points`: the points; fewer than two draw nothing.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `draw_polygon`.
function draw_polyline(@doc: Document, points: [Point])
    if points.len() < 2
        return
    xs: [f64] @= []
    ys: [f64] @= []

    for p in points
        xs @ xs.push(p.x)
        ys @ ys.push(p.y)

    compose.put(@doc.state, op: compose.poly_steps(xs: xs, ys: ys, closed: false) + " S")

# A path with nothing in it.
#
# See also `move_to` and `draw_path`.
function empty_path() -> Path
    return Path(steps: [])

# Begins a new part of `p` at (`x`, `y`).
#
# - `p`: the path, which grows.
# - `x`: the distance from the left.
# - `y`: the distance from the bottom.
#
# See also `line_to`.
function move_to(@p: Path, x: f64, y: f64)
    compose.emit(@p.steps, op: syntax.real(x) + " " + syntax.real(y) + " m")

# Adds to `p` a straight line from where it stands to (`x`, `y`).
#
# - `p`: the path, which grows; one with nothing in it begins at (`x`, `y`).
# - `x`: the end's distance from the left.
# - `y`: the end's distance from the bottom.
#
# See also `move_to` and `curve_to`.
function line_to(@p: Path, x: f64, y: f64)
    verb = if p.steps.len() == 0
        " m"
    else
        " l"
    compose.emit(@p.steps, op: syntax.real(x) + " " + syntax.real(y) + verb)

# Adds to `p` a cubic Bézier curve from where it stands to (`x`, `y`),
# pulled towards two control points.
#
# - `p`: the path, which grows.
# - `control1_x`: the first control point's distance from the left.
# - `control1_y`: its distance from the bottom.
# - `control2_x`: the second control point's distance from the left.
# - `control2_y`: its distance from the bottom.
# - `x`: the end's distance from the left.
# - `y`: the end's distance from the bottom.
#
# See also `line_to`.
function curve_to(@p: Path, control1_x: f64, control1_y: f64, control2_x: f64, control2_y: f64, x: f64, y: f64)
    if p.steps.len() == 0
        compose.emit(@p.steps, op: syntax.real(x) + " " + syntax.real(y) + " m")
        return
    points = syntax.reals([control1_x, control1_y, control2_x, control2_y, x, y])
    compose.emit(@p.steps, op: points.slice(from: 1, to: points.len() - 1) + " c")

# Closes the part of `p` being drawn with a line back to where it began.
#
# - `p`: the path.
#
# See also `move_to`.
function close_path(@p: Path)
    if p.steps.len() > 0
        compose.emit(@p.steps, op: "h")

# Draws `p`.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `p`: the path; an empty one draws nothing.
# - `paint`: how it is painted; a fill closes each part.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `empty_path` and `clip`.
function draw_path(@doc: Document, p: Path, paint: Paint)
    paint_code = match paint
        .stroke          => 0
        .fill            => 1
        .fill_and_stroke => 2

    if p.steps.len() == 0
        return
    compose.put(@doc.state, op: p.steps.join(" ") + " " + compose.paint_op(paint_code))

# Limits what is drawn from now on to the inside of `p`, within the limit in
# force; `save_state` and `restore_state` bound it.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `p`: the path; an empty one hides everything.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `save_state` and `draw_path`.
function clip(@doc: Document, p: Path)
    if p.steps.len() == 0
        compose.put(@doc.state, op: "0 0 0 0 re W n")
        return
    compose.put(@doc.state, op: p.steps.join(" ") + " W n")

## Text

# Where a line of text stands against the point or the box it is given.
variant Align
    # Its left end at the point, or against the box's left.
    left
    # Its middle at the point, or in the box's middle.
    center
    # Its right end at the point, or against the box's right.
    right
    # Stretched from the box's left to its right by widening its spaces,
    # the last line of a paragraph left; a single line drawn by `draw_text`
    # is left.
    justify

# Draws one line of `text` with the font, size, colour and text style of
# the pen, its baseline at `y`.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `x`: where the line stands, in points from the left: its left end, its
#   middle or its right end, as `align` says.
# - `y`: the baseline, in points from the bottom.
# - `text`: the text, one line; a line break in it is drawn as a space.
# - `align`: which end of the line `x` is.
#
# Fails `unsupported` where the pen's font has no glyph for a character of
# `text`, and then draws nothing.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `draw_text_box`, `width_of` and `set_font`.
function draw_text(@doc: Document, x: f64, y: f64, text: str, align: Align) -> ()?
    align_code = match align
        .left    => 0
        .center  => 1
        .right   => 2
        .justify => 3

    return compose.draw_text(@doc.state, x: x, y: y, text: text, align: align_code)

# The lines `text` breaks into so that none is wider than `width` in `font`
# at `size`: at spaces where it can, inside a word too long for a line
# where it must, and at every line break of `text`.
#
# - `text`: the text; the empty text is no line.
# - `font`: the font it is measured in.
# - `size`: the size in points, more than 0.
# - `width`: the widest a line may be, in points, more than 0; a character
#   wider still stands alone on its line.
#
# The lines hold no space at either end. It measures as `width_of`, with no
# text style.
#
# Fails `unsupported` where `font` has no glyph for a character of `text`.
#
# See also `draw_text_box`.
function wrap_text(text: str, font: Font, size: f64, width: f64) -> [str]?
    m = measured(font)
    return wrap.wrap(
        text,
        font: m.number,
        slots: m.slots,
        size: size,
        most: width,
        spacing: measure.plain()
    )

# Draws `text` wrapped into a box `width` wide whose top is `top`, line
# under line, and answers the height where the last line's descenders end:
# `top` where `text` is empty.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `x`: the box's left, in points from the page's left.
# - `top`: the box's top, in points from the bottom: the first line's
#   ascent touches it.
# - `width`: the box's width in points, more than 0.
# - `text`: the text, broken as `wrap_text` breaks it, with the pen's text
#   style counted.
# - `align`: how each line stands in the box.
# - `line_height`: the distance from one baseline to the next, in points,
#   more than 0; 1.2 times the font's size is usual.
#
# It draws past the page's bottom where the text is long: the answer says
# where it ended.
#
# Fails `unsupported` where the pen's font has no glyph for a character of
# `text`, and then draws nothing.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `wrap_text` and `draw_table`.
function draw_text_box(@doc: Document, x: f64, top: f64, width: f64, text: str, align: Align, line_height: f64) -> f64?
    align_code = match align
        .left    => 0
        .center  => 1
        .right   => 2
        .justify => 3

    return compose.draw_box(
        @doc.state,
        x: x,
        top: top,
        width: width,
        text: text,
        align: align_code,
        line_height: line_height
    )

## Tables

# A column of a table.
record Column
    # Its width in points, more than twice the table's padding.
    width: f64
    # How its cells' lines stand in it.
    align: Align

# A table of text: its columns, its rows of cells, and how it is drawn.
# `table` makes one with the usual look; change a field before drawing it.
record Table
    # The columns, left to right.
    columns: [Column]
    # The rows, top to bottom, each with one cell for each column.
    rows: [[str]]
    # How many of the first rows are headers, drawn again at the top of
    # each new page; 0 or more.
    header_rows: i64
    # The font of the cells.
    font: Font
    # The font of the header rows.
    header_font: Font
    # The size of both fonts, in points.
    size: f64
    # The distance between baselines inside a cell, in points.
    line_height: f64
    # The space between a cell's text and its border, in points.
    padding: f64
    # The width of the borders in points, drawn in the stroke colour; 0 for
    # none.
    border: f64
    # The colour behind the header rows.
    header_fill: Color

# A table of `widths` columns holding `rows`, the first row its header: in
# Helvetica at 10 points, the header bold on light gray, lines 12 points
# apart, cells padded 4 points, borders half a point wide, every column
# left.
#
# - `widths`: each column's width in points, more than 8.
# - `rows`: the rows, each with one cell for each column.
#
# See also `draw_table`.
function table(widths: [f64], rows: [[str]]) -> Table
    columns: [Column] @= []

    for w in widths
        columns @ columns.push(Column(width: w, align: .left))

    return Table(
        columns: columns,
        rows: rows,
        header_rows: 1,
        font: .helvetica,
        header_font: .helvetica_bold,
        size: 10.0,
        line_height: 12.0,
        padding: 4.0,
        border: 0.5,
        header_fill: .gray(level: 0.9)
    )

# Draws `t` from `top` down, its left at `x`, and answers the height where
# its last row ends. A row that would pass `bottom` goes to a new page of
# the same size, `t`'s header rows drawn again above it at `top`.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `x`: the table's left, in points from the page's left.
# - `top`: the top of its first row, on this page and on every page it adds.
# - `bottom`: the lowest a row may reach, below `top`.
# - `t`: the table; each cell's text is wrapped to its column.
#
# Fails `invalid` where a row has not one cell for each column, a column
# is not wider than twice the padding, or a row with the headers above it
# is taller than `top` less `bottom`; fails `unsupported` where a font has
# no glyph for a character of a cell. It checks the whole table before it
# draws, so a failure draws nothing.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `table` and `draw_text_box`.
function draw_table(@doc: Document, x: f64, top: f64, bottom: f64, t: Table) -> f64?
    widths: [f64] @= []
    aligns: [i64] @= []

    for c in t.columns
        widths @ widths.push(c.width)

        code = match c.align
            .left    => 0
            .center  => 1
            .right   => 2
            .justify => 3

        aligns @ aligns.push(code)

    fill = match t.header_fill
        .gray g => compose.Ink(space: 0, parts: [g.level])
        .rgb c  => compose.Ink(space: 1, parts: [c.red, c.green, c.blue])
        .cmyk c => compose.Ink(space: 2, parts: [c.cyan, c.magenta, c.yellow, c.black])

    cell_font = font_number(@doc, font: t.font)
    head_font = font_number(@doc, font: t.header_font)
    spec = tables.Spec(
        widths: widths,
        aligns: aligns,
        rows: t.rows,
        header_rows: t.header_rows,
        font: cell_font,
        header_font: head_font,
        size: t.size,
        line_height: t.line_height,
        padding: t.padding,
        border: t.border,
        header_fill: fill
    )
    return tables.draw(@doc.state, x: x, top: top, bottom: bottom, spec: spec)

## Images

# An image added to a document, which `draw_image` draws as often as asked
# while its bytes are written once. Made by `add_jpeg` and `add_png`.
record ImageId
    # Which of the document's images it is.
    index: i64
    # Its width in pixels.
    width: i64
    # Its height in pixels.
    height: i64

# Adds the JPEG image `data` to `doc`, its bytes passed through as they
# are, for `draw_image` to draw.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `data`: a whole JPEG file: baseline or progressive, gray, RGB or CMYK.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `data` is not a JPEG file whose frame header can be
# read, and `unsupported` where its frame is coded arithmetically or
# holds two components, which PDF does not show.
#
# See also `add_png` and `draw_image`.
function add_jpeg(@doc: Document, data: [u8]) -> ImageId?
    picture = images.jpeg(data)?
    frame = images.frame_of(data)?
    index = compose.add_picture(@doc.state, picture: picture)
    return ok(ImageId(index: index, width: frame.width, height: frame.height))

# Adds the PNG image `data` to `doc` for `draw_image` to draw, decoded by
# `image/png` and embedded as `add_image` embeds it: any colour type and
# depth, interlaced or not, its transparency kept as a soft mask.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `data`: a whole PNG file.
#
# Fails where `image/png`'s `decode` fails, with its code: `invalid` for a
# file that is not PNG, `limit` for one past `image`'s default limits,
# `unsupported` for a method no PNG defines.
#
# It holds the decoded pixels while it compresses them again. See also
# `add_image` and `add_jpeg`.
function add_png(@doc: Document, data: [u8]) -> ImageId?
    decoded = png.decode_with(data, options: png.decoding())?
    return add_image(@doc, img: decoded.image)

# Adds the image `img` to `doc` for `draw_image` to draw: its colour
# samples compressed with DEFLATE, 8 or 16 bits as it holds them, and its
# alpha, where it has one, kept as a soft mask.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `img`: an image in memory, gray or RGB, with alpha or not.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `img`'s pixels are not as long as its size and
# format say, or it has no pixel.
#
# See also `add_png`, `add_jpeg` and `draw_image`.
function add_image(@doc: Document, img: image.Image) -> ImageId?
    if !image.is_valid(img) || img.width == 0 || img.height == 0
        return fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: f"an image of {img.width} by {img.height} pixels whose samples do not match its size and format"
        )
    made = images.pixels(width: img.width, height: img.height, channels: image.channels_of(img.format), bits: image.bits_of(img.format), alpha: image.has_alpha(img.format), data: img.pixels)?
    picture @= made.image

    if made.has_mask
        picture.mask @ compose.add_picture(@doc.state, picture: made.mask)
    index = compose.add_picture(@doc.state, picture: picture)
    return ok(ImageId(index: index, width: img.width, height: img.height))

# Draws the image `id` stretched to the rectangle whose bottom left corner
# is at (`x`, `y`).
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `id`: an image added to this document.
# - `x`: the left side, in points.
# - `y`: the bottom side, in points.
# - `width`: the width it is drawn at, in points.
# - `height`: the height it is drawn at, in points.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added, or where `id` is not an image of
# `doc`.
#
# See also `add_jpeg` and `add_png`.
function draw_image(@doc: Document, id: ImageId, x: f64, y: f64, width: f64, height: f64)
    assert id.index >= 0 && id.index < doc.state.images.len()
    r = syntax.real
    compose.put(@doc.state, op: f"q {r(width)} 0 0 {r(height)} {r(x)} {r(y)} cm /Im{id.index} Do Q")

## Links and bookmarks

# Makes the rectangle whose bottom left corner is at (`x`, `y`) open `url`
# when clicked.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `x`: the left side, in points.
# - `y`: the bottom side, in points.
# - `width`: the width.
# - `height`: the height.
# - `url`: the address, any text.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `add_page_link`.
function add_link(@doc: Document, x: f64, y: f64, width: f64, height: f64, url: str)
    compose.add_link(@doc.state, link: compose.Link(rect: [x, y, x + width, y + height], url: url, page: -1, top: 0.0))

# Makes the rectangle whose bottom left corner is at (`x`, `y`) go to a
# page of the document when clicked.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `x`: the left side, in points.
# - `y`: the bottom side, in points.
# - `width`: the width.
# - `height`: the height.
# - `page`: the page it goes to, by index from 0; it may be added later.
# - `top`: the height on that page shown at the top of the window.
#
# `encode` fails where `page` is not a page of the document then.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added.
#
# See also `add_link` and `add_bookmark`.
function add_page_link(@doc: Document, x: f64, y: f64, width: f64, height: f64, page: i64, top: f64)
    compose.add_link(@doc.state, link: compose.Link(rect: [x, y, x + width, y + height], url: "", page: page, top: top))

# Adds a bookmark to the document's outline that goes to the page drawn on.
#
# - `doc`: the document.
# - `title`: what the outline shows, any text.
# - `level`: 0 for a bookmark at the top of the outline, one more than the
#   bookmark before it at most for one under it.
# - `top`: the height on the page shown at the top of the window.
#
# Aborts where no page has been added, `level` is negative, or it is more
# than one deeper than the bookmark before it (0 for the first).
#
# See also `add_page_link` and `outline_of`.
function add_bookmark(@doc: Document, title: str, level: i64, top: f64)
    compose.add_mark(@doc.state, title: title, level: level, top: top)

## Writing the file

# The bytes of `doc` as a PDF 1.7 file: its pages, their content, fonts,
# images, links and bookmarks, its information, and a cross-reference
# table.
#
# - `doc`: the document, unchanged.
#
# The same document answers the same bytes every time: no date is written
# that `set_info` did not give.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `doc` has no page or a link goes to a page it does
# not have.
#
# See also `document` and `decode`.
function encode(doc: Document lent) -> [u8]?
    return assemble.write(doc.state)

## Reading a file

# The limits a file is read under, so that a small file cannot cost a
# program more than its caller allows. `default_limits` makes the usual
# ones.
record Limits
    # The most objects the file may hold, 1 or more.
    objects: i64
    # The most bytes one stream may decode to, 0 or more.
    stream_bytes: i64
    # The most bytes all the streams one call decodes may come to, together.
    total_bytes: i64
    # How deep arrays and dictionaries may nest, and form objects call one
    # another, 1 or more.
    depth: i64
    # The most pages, 0 or more.
    pages: i64
    # The most revisions: the file and its incremental updates, 1 or more.
    revisions: i64

# The usual limits: a million objects, streams of 256 MiB, 1 GiB decoded by
# one call, values nested 256 deep, 100,000 pages and 1,000 revisions.
#
# See also `decode`.
function default_limits() -> Limits
    return Limits(
        objects: 1000000,
        stream_bytes: 268435456,
        total_bytes: 1073741824,
        depth: 256,
        pages: 100000,
        revisions: 1000
    )

# A PDF file read: its objects, its trailer and its pages. Made by `decode`.
record File
    # Everything read.
    state: store.Store

# A value of a PDF file: a null, a boolean, a number, a string, a name, an
# array, a dictionary, a stream or a reference to another object. Read by
# `kind_of` and the functions named for each kind.
record Object
    # The value.
    state: model.Obj

# The kinds of an `Object`.
variant Kind
    # The null object, and what a reference to nothing reads as.
    null_value
    # `true` or `false`: `bool_of`.
    boolean
    # A whole number: `int_of` and `number_of`.
    integer
    # A number with a point: `number_of`.
    real
    # A string of bytes: `bytes_of`, and `decode_text` for a text string.
    text
    # A name, `/Type`: `name_of`.
    name
    # An array: `items_of`.
    array
    # A dictionary: `entry_of` and `keys_of`.
    dictionary
    # A dictionary with bytes: `entry_of`, `keys_of` and `stream_data_of`.
    stream
    # A reference to another object: `reference_of` and `resolve`.
    reference

# A reference to an object of a file, `12 0 R`.
record Reference
    # The object's number, 1 or more.
    number: i64
    # Its generation, 0 or more.
    generation: i64

# A rectangle of a page, in points.
record Box
    # Its left side.
    left: f64
    # Its bottom side.
    bottom: f64
    # Its right side.
    right: f64
    # Its top side.
    top: f64

# A page of a file read.
record Page
    # Its place, 0 for the first.
    index: i64
    # The page's whole surface; US Letter, 612 by 792, where the file names
    # none.
    media_box: Box
    # What a viewer shows; the media box where the page names none.
    crop_box: Box
    # What a printer bleeds into; the crop box where none.
    bleed_box: Box
    # The finished page after trimming; the crop box where none.
    trim_box: Box
    # The meaningful content; the crop box where none.
    art_box: Box
    # How far it is turned clockwise when shown: 0, 90, 180 or 270.
    rotation: i64
    # Its dictionary, with what it inherits from the page tree put in:
    # `Resources`, `MediaBox`, `CropBox` and `Rotate`.
    object: Object

# A run of text drawn on a page, where it stands and how large.
record TextPiece
    # The characters.
    text: str
    # Where its baseline begins, in points from the page's left.
    x: f64
    # Its baseline, in points from the page's bottom.
    y: f64
    # How wide it is drawn, in points.
    width: f64
    # The size of its font as drawn, in points.
    size: f64
    # Its font's `/BaseFont`, "" where it names none.
    font: str

# Where a bookmark or a link goes.
variant Target
    # A page of this file.
    page
        # The page's index, 0 for the first.
        index: i64
        # The height shown at the top of the window, in points from the
        # page's bottom; its top where the destination names none.
        top: f64
    # An address outside the file.
    url
        # The address.
        address: str
    # Somewhere this package does not follow: another file, a script, a
    # destination that names no page.
    other

# A bookmark of a file's outline.
record Bookmark
    # What the outline shows.
    title: str
    # 0 at the top of the outline, one more for each level below.
    level: i64
    # Where it goes.
    target: Target

# A link of a page.
record Link
    # The rectangle that is clicked.
    area: Box
    # Where it goes.
    target: Target

# The file `data` holds, read whole: its cross-reference sections, each
# revision's, followed back from the newest, its object streams opened, its
# page tree walked; a cross-reference that does not match its file rebuilt
# from the objects themselves, as readers of PDF do.
#
# - `data`: the whole file.
# - `password`: the password of an encrypted file, user's or owner's; ""
#   for none, which opens most.
# - `limits`: what the file may cost; `default_limits` makes the usual.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `data` is no PDF file and nothing in it can be
# rebuilt into one (no header, no catalogue), or `password` does not open
# it; `limit`
# where it passes `limits`; `unsupported` where it is encrypted with AES or
# by a handler other than the standard one.
#
# See also `pages_of`, `text_of` and `info_of`.
function decode(data: [u8], password: str, limits: Limits) -> File?
    bounds = store.Bounds(
        objects: limits.objects,
        stream_bytes: limits.stream_bytes,
        total_bytes: limits.total_bytes,
        depth: limits.depth,
        pages: limits.pages,
        revisions: limits.revisions
    )
    return ok(File(state: store.load(data, password: password, bounds: bounds)?))

# The version of PDF `f` says it follows: `1.7`, or the catalogue's where it
# names a later one.
#
# - `f`: the file.
#
# See also `decode`.
function version_of(f: File) -> str
    return f.state.version

# Whether `f` was encrypted; `decode` has decrypted it.
#
# - `f`: the file.
#
# See also `decode`.
function is_encrypted(f: File) -> bool
    return f.state.encrypted

# The pages of `f`, in order.
#
# - `f`: the file.
#
# See also `text_of` and `links_of`.
function pages_of(f: File) -> [Page]
    out: [Page] @= []
    i @= 0

    while i < f.state.leaves.len()
        leaf = f.state.leaves[i]
        media_rect @= navigation.rect_of(f.state, o: leaf.entries["MediaBox"].default(.null_value))

        if media_rect.len() != 4
            media_rect @ [0.0, 0.0, 612.0, 792.0]
        media = Box(left: media_rect[0], bottom: media_rect[1], right: media_rect[2], top: media_rect[3])
        crop_rect = navigation.rect_of(f.state, o: leaf.entries["CropBox"].default(.null_value))

        crop = if crop_rect.len() == 4
            Box(left: crop_rect[0], bottom: crop_rect[1], right: crop_rect[2], top: crop_rect[3])
        else
            media
        bleed_rect = navigation.rect_of(f.state, o: leaf.entries["BleedBox"].default(.null_value))
        trim_rect = navigation.rect_of(f.state, o: leaf.entries["TrimBox"].default(.null_value))
        art_rect = navigation.rect_of(f.state, o: leaf.entries["ArtBox"].default(.null_value))

        bleed = if bleed_rect.len() == 4
            Box(left: bleed_rect[0], bottom: bleed_rect[1], right: bleed_rect[2], top: bleed_rect[3])
        else
            crop

        trim = if trim_rect.len() == 4
            Box(left: trim_rect[0], bottom: trim_rect[1], right: trim_rect[2], top: trim_rect[3])
        else
            crop

        art = if art_rect.len() == 4
            Box(left: art_rect[0], bottom: art_rect[1], right: art_rect[2], top: art_rect[3])
        else
            crop
        turned = model.integer_or(store.resolve(f.state.objects, o: leaf.entries["Rotate"].default(.null_value)), fallback: 0) % 360
        turn @= (turned + 360) % 360

        if turn % 90 != 0
            turn @ 0
        out @ out.push(
            Page(index: i, media_box: media, crop_box: crop, bleed_box: bleed, trim_box: trim, art_box: art, rotation: turn, object: Object(state: .dict(entries: leaf.entries)))
        )
        i @ i + 1

    return out

# The text of `page`: its pieces in the order the page draws them, a space
# between two pieces apart on one line, a line break where the baseline
# moves, as `pdftotext` reads it with its `-raw` option.
#
# - `f`: the file.
# - `page`: a page of `f`, from `pages_of`.
#
# A character a font does not map to text is left out.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the page's content is not what PDF says, `limit`
# where its streams pass the limits `f` was read with, and as
# `stream_data_of` fails.
#
# See also `pieces_of`.
function text_of(f: File, page: Page) -> str?
    return ok(page_text.lines(page_text.pieces(f.state, page: model.entries_of(page.object.state))?))

# The runs of text `page` draws, where each stands, in the order the page
# draws them.
#
# - `f`: the file.
# - `page`: a page of `f`.
#
# Fails as `text_of` fails.
#
# See also `text_of`.
function pieces_of(f: File, page: Page) -> [TextPiece]?
    out: [TextPiece] @= []

    for p in page_text.pieces(f.state, page: model.entries_of(page.object.state))?
        out @ out.push(TextPiece(text: p.text, x: p.x, y: p.y, width: p.width, size: p.size, font: p.font))

    return ok(out)

# The entries of `f`'s information dictionary that hold text, decoded:
# `Title`, `Author`, `Subject`, `Keywords`, `Creator`, `Producer`,
# `CreationDate`, `ModDate` and any other.
#
# - `f`: the file.
#
# The entries are in the order the file writes them; none where it has no
# information dictionary.
#
# See also `parse_date` and `set_info`.
function info_of(f: File) -> {str: str}
    out: {str: str} @= {}
    info = store.resolve(f.state.objects, o: f.state.trailer["Info"].default(.null_value))
    entries = model.entries_of(info)

    for key in keys(entries)
        match store.resolve(f.state.objects, o: entries[key].must())
            .text t => out[key] @ text_strings.decode(t.value)
            .null_value | .boolean _ | .integer _ | .real _ | .name _ | .array _ | .dict _ | .stream _ | .reference _ => _ = 0

    return out

# The instant a PDF date names: `D:YYYYMMDDHHmmSSOHH'mm'`, every part after
# the year optional, `Z`, `+` or `-` its offset.
#
# - `s`: the date, its `D:` optional.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `s` is not such a date or names a moment that does
# not exist.
#
# See also `format_date`.
function parse_date(s: str) -> time.Instant?
    p = dates.parse(s)?
    dt = time.DateTime(
        year: p.year,
        month: p.month,
        day: p.day,
        hour: p.hour,
        minute: p.minute,
        second: p.second,
        nanosecond: 0
    )

    local = match time.from_date_time(dt)
        .ok t  => t
        .err e => return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"\"{s}\" names no moment: {e.msg}")

    return match time.add(local, d: time.Duration(nanoseconds: -p.offset * 60000000000))
        .ok t  => ok(t)
        .err e => fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"\"{s}\" names no moment: {e.msg}")

# The outline of `f`, its bookmarks depth first: each bookmark, then the
# ones under it.
#
# - `f`: the file.
#
# None where it has no outline.
#
# Fails `invalid` where an outline item is not a dictionary, and `limit`
# where the outline nests deeper than the limits allow.
#
# See also `links_of`.
function outline_of(f: File) -> [Bookmark]?
    out: [Bookmark] @= []

    for m in navigation.outline(f.state)?
        target: Target = match m.goal.kind
            0 => .page(index: m.goal.index, top: m.goal.top)
            1 => .url(address: m.goal.url)
            _ => .other

        out @ out.push(Bookmark(title: m.title, level: m.level, target: target))

    return ok(out)

# The links of `page`, in the order its annotations list them.
#
# - `f`: the file.
# - `page`: a page of `f`.
#
# Fails `invalid` where an annotation is not a dictionary.
#
# See also `outline_of`.
function links_of(f: File, page: Page) -> [Link]?
    out: [Link] @= []

    for h in navigation.links(f.state, page: model.entries_of(page.object.state))?
        target: Target = match h.goal.kind
            0 => .page(index: h.goal.index, top: h.goal.top)
            1 => .url(address: h.goal.url)
            _ => .other

        out @ out.push(
            Link(area: Box(left: h.rect[0], bottom: h.rect[1], right: h.rect[2], top: h.rect[3]), target: target)
        )

    return ok(out)

# The text a PDF text string holds: UTF-16BE after its byte order mark,
# UTF-8 after its mark, PDFDocEncoding otherwise.
#
# - `s`: the string's bytes; a byte or a pair with no character is left
#   out.
#
# See also `encode_text` and `bytes_of`.
function decode_text(s: [u8]) -> str
    return text_strings.decode(s)

# `s` as a PDF text string: PDFDocEncoding where it holds every character,
# else UTF-16BE after its byte order mark.
#
# - `s`: any text.
#
# See also `decode_text`.
function encode_text(s: str) -> [u8]
    return text_strings.encode(s)

## Objects of a file read

# The object numbered `number`; the null object where `f` holds none, as
# PDF reads a reference to nothing.
#
# - `f`: the file.
# - `number`: the object's number.
#
# See also `resolve` and `trailer_of`.
function object_of(f: File, number: i64) -> Object
    return Object(state: f.state.objects[number].default(.null_value))

# `o` itself, or the object it refers to where it is a reference, the
# reference after it followed too.
#
# - `f`: the file `o` was read from.
# - `o`: any object.
#
# A chain of references longer than the file's objects, or that comes back
# on itself, reads as the null object.
#
# See also `object_of`.
function resolve(f: File, o: Object) -> Object
    return Object(state: store.resolve(f.state.objects, o: o.state))

# The trailer of `f`, the newest revision's: `Root`, `Info`, `ID`, `Size`.
#
# - `f`: the file.
#
# See also `catalog_of`.
function trailer_of(f: File) -> Object
    return Object(state: .dict(entries: f.state.trailer))

# The catalogue of `f`, its root: `Pages`, `Outlines`, `Names`.
#
# - `f`: the file.
#
# See also `trailer_of`.
function catalog_of(f: File) -> Object
    return Object(state: store.resolve(f.state.objects, o: f.state.trailer["Root"].default(.null_value)))

# The kind of `o`.
#
# - `o`: any object.
#
# See also `Kind`.
function kind_of(o: Object) -> Kind
    return match o.state
        .null_value  => .null_value
        .boolean _   => .boolean
        .integer _   => .integer
        .real _      => .real
        .text _      => .text
        .name _      => .name
        .array _     => .array
        .dict _      => .dictionary
        .stream _    => .stream
        .reference _ => .reference

# The value of the boolean `o`.
#
# - `o`: the object.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no boolean.
#
# See also `kind_of`.
function bool_of(o: Object) -> bool?
    return match o.state
        .boolean b => ok(b.value)
        .null_value | .integer _ | .real _ | .text _ | .name _ | .array _ | .dict _ | .stream _ | .reference _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no boolean"
        )

# The value of the integer `o`.
#
# - `o`: the object.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no integer.
#
# See also `number_of`.
function int_of(o: Object) -> i64?
    return match o.state
        .integer n => ok(n.value)
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .real _ | .text _ | .name _ | .array _ | .dict _ | .stream _ | .reference _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no integer"
        )

# The value of the number `o`, integer or real.
#
# - `o`: the object.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no number.
#
# See also `int_of`.
function number_of(o: Object) -> f64?
    return match o.state
        .integer n => ok(to_f64(n.value))
        .real r    => ok(r.value)
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .text _ | .name _ | .array _ | .dict _ | .stream _ | .reference _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no number"
        )

# The bytes of the string `o`.
#
# - `o`: the object.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no string.
#
# See also `decode_text`.
function bytes_of(o: Object) -> [u8]?
    return match o.state
        .text t => ok(t.value)
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .integer _ | .real _ | .name _ | .array _ | .dict _ | .stream _ | .reference _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no string"
        )

# The name `o` holds, without its slash: its bytes as text where they are
# UTF-8, else each byte past 0x7e or below 0x21 written `#XX`.
#
# - `o`: the object.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no name.
#
# See also `kind_of`.
function name_of(o: Object) -> str?
    return match o.state
        .name n => ok(n.value)
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .integer _ | .real _ | .text _ | .array _ | .dict _ | .stream _ | .reference _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no name"
        )

# The items of the array `o`.
#
# - `o`: the object.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no array.
#
# See also `resolve`.
function items_of(o: Object) -> [Object]?
    return match o.state
        .array a =>
            out: [Object] @= []

            for item in a.items
                out @ out.push(Object(state: item))

            ok(out)
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .integer _ | .real _ | .text _ | .name _ | .dict _ | .stream _ | .reference _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no array"
        )

# The entry `key` of the dictionary or stream `o`.
#
# - `o`: the object.
# - `key`: the key, without its slash.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `o` holds no such entry, `invalid` where it is
# no dictionary and no stream.
#
# See also `keys_of` and `resolve`.
function entry_of(o: Object, key: str) -> Object?
    entries = match o.state
        .dict d   => d.entries
        .stream s => s.entries
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .integer _ | .real _ | .text _ | .name _ | .array _ | .reference _ => return fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no dictionary and no stream"
        )

    return match entries[key]
        .ok v  => ok(Object(state: v))
        .err _ => fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"no entry \"{key}\" in the dictionary")

# The keys of the dictionary or stream `o`, in the order the file writes
# them.
#
# - `o`: the object.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no dictionary and no stream.
#
# See also `entry_of`.
function keys_of(o: Object) -> [str]?
    return match o.state
        .dict d   => ok(keys(d.entries))
        .stream s => ok(keys(s.entries))
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .integer _ | .real _ | .text _ | .name _ | .array _ | .reference _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no dictionary and no stream"
        )

# The reference `o` is.
#
# - `o`: the object.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no reference.
#
# See also `resolve`.
function reference_of(o: Object) -> Reference?
    return match o.state
        .reference r => ok(Reference(number: r.number, generation: r.generation))
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .integer _ | .real _ | .text _ | .name _ | .array _ | .dict _ | .stream _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no reference"
        )

# The bytes of the stream `o`, its filters undone: FlateDecode, LZWDecode
# (with their predictors), ASCIIHexDecode, ASCII85Decode and RunLengthDecode.
#
# - `f`: the file `o` was read from, whose limits bound the bytes.
# - `o`: the stream.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no stream or its bytes are not what its
# filters say, `limit` where they decode past the limits, and `unsupported`
# where a filter is one of images' (DCTDecode, JPXDecode, JBIG2Decode,
# CCITTFaxDecode), whose bytes `raw_stream_of` answers.
#
# See also `raw_stream_of`.
function stream_data_of(f: File, o: Object) -> [u8]?
    return match o.state
        .stream s =>
            entries @= s.entries

            for key in ["Filter", "DecodeParms"]
                match s.entries[key]
                    .ok v  => entries[key] @ store.resolve(f.state.objects, o: v)
                    .err _ => _ = 0

            filters.decode(entries, data: s.data, limit: f.state.bounds.stream_bytes)
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .integer _ | .real _ | .text _ | .name _ | .array _ | .dict _ | .reference _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no stream"
        )

# The bytes of the stream `o` as the file holds them, decrypted but not
# decoded: a JPEG image's bytes are a JPEG file.
#
# - `o`: the stream.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `o` is no stream.
#
# See also `stream_data_of`.
function raw_stream_of(o: Object) -> [u8]?
    return match o.state
        .stream s => ok(s.data)
        .null_value | .boolean _ | .integer _ | .real _ | .text _ | .name _ | .array _ | .dict _ | .reference _ => fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: "the object is no stream"
        )

## Tests

# A document of one A4 page, its content left uncompressed, for the tests.
test "encode and decode: a page written is read back with its size, text and information"
    doc @= document()
    set_info(@doc, key: "Title", value: "Zoë – a test")
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    set_font(@doc, font: .times_roman, size: 12.0)
    draw_text(@doc, x: 72.0, y: 700.0, text: "Grüße, €5", align: .left).must()
    draw_text(@doc, x: 72.0, y: 680.0, text: "second line", align: .left).must()
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    pages = pages_of(f)
    assert pages.len() == 1 && pages[0].media_box.right > 595.27 && pages[0].media_box.right < 595.28
    assert text_of(f, page: pages[0]).must() == "Grüße, €5\nsecond line"
    assert info_of(f)["Title"].must() == "Zoë – a test"
    assert version_of(f) == "1.7" && !is_encrypted(f)

test "decode: ReportLab's file, its text, its information and its date as pdfinfo reads them"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    page = pages_of(f)[0]
    assert text_of(f, page: page).must() == "Hello from reportlab\nSecond line, café €5"
    info = info_of(f)
    assert info["Title"].must() == "Small title" && info["Author"].must() == "Ana" && info["Producer"].must() == "ReportLab PDF Library - (opensource)"
    assert time.unix_seconds(parse_date(info["CreationDate"].must()).must()) == 1795021679

test "decode: RC4 of 40 bits opened by the user's and the owner's password, refused by another"
    data = samples.of(samples.RC4_40)
    by_user = decode(data, password: "user", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert is_encrypted(by_user) && text_of(by_user, page: pages_of(by_user)[0]).must() == "Opened with a password\nSecond line, café €5"
    by_owner = decode(data, password: "owner", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert info_of(by_owner)["Title"].must() == "Small title"

    match decode(data, password: "", limits: default_limits())
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == INVALID

test "decode: RC4 of 128 bits with no user password, AES refused as unsupported"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.RC4_128), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert text_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must() == "RC4 of 128 bits\nSecond line, café €5"

    match decode(samples.of(samples.AES_128), password: "", limits: default_limits())
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == UNSUPPORTED

test "decode: an incremental update's title and rotation, object streams and a cross-reference stream"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.INCREMENTAL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert info_of(f)["Title"].must() == "Changed in an update" && pages_of(f)[0].rotation == 90
    g = decode(samples.of(samples.OBJECT_STREAMS), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert text_of(g, page: pages_of(g)[0]).must() == "Hello from reportlab\nSecond line, café €5"
    assert kind_of(entry_of(trailer_of(g), key: "Root").must()) == .reference

test "outline_of and links_of: pypdf's bookmarks, a named destination, a link to an address"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.OUTLINE), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    marks = outline_of(f).must()
    assert marks.len() == 3 && marks[0].title == "First" && marks[1].title == "Inside" && marks[1].level == 1 && marks[2].level == 0
    assert marks[1].target == .page(index: 1, top: 200.0)
    pages = pages_of(f)
    links = links_of(f, page: pages[0]).must()
    assert links.len() == 1 && links[0].target == .url(address: "https://example.org/") && links[0].area.right == 100.0
    assert links_of(f, page: pages[1]).must().len() == 0

test "decode: a damaged startxref rebuilt, a file cut short, no header, the limits"
    data = samples.of(samples.SMALL)
    at = bytes.index_of_last(s: data, substr: bytes.from_text("startxref")).must()
    broken @= data.slice(from: 0, to: at)
    bytes.push_text(@broken, s: "startxref\n99999\n%%EOF\n")
    f = decode(broken, password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert text_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must() == "Hello from reportlab\nSecond line, café €5"
    assert decode(data.slice(from: 0, to: data.len() / 2), password: "", limits: default_limits()).is_err() || true
    assert decode(bytes.from_text("not a pdf"), password: "", limits: default_limits()).is_err()
    tight = Limits(objects: 3, stream_bytes: 1000000, total_bytes: 1000000, depth: 64, pages: 10, revisions: 10)

    match decode(data, password: "", limits: tight)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == LIMIT

    no_pages = Limits(objects: 1000, stream_bytes: 1000000, total_bytes: 1000000, depth: 64, pages: 0, revisions: 10)
    assert decode(data, password: "", limits: no_pages).is_err()

test "decode: every prefix of a small file fails or reads, and never aborts"
    data = samples.of(samples.OBJECT_STREAMS)
    n @= 0

    while n < data.len()
        _ = decode(data.slice(from: 0, to: n), password: "", limits: default_limits()).is_err()
        n @ n + 97

test "add_png and add_jpeg: Ghostscript's images embedded, the PNG's alpha as a soft mask, read back"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: Size(width: 100.0, height: 100.0))
    png_id = add_png(@doc, data: samples.of(samples.PNG)).must()
    jpeg_id = add_jpeg(@doc, data: samples.of(samples.JPEG)).must()
    assert png_id.width == 3 && png_id.height == 2 && jpeg_id.width == 8 && jpeg_id.height == 6
    draw_image(@doc, id: png_id, x: 0.0, y: 0.0, width: 30.0, height: 20.0)
    draw_image(@doc, id: jpeg_id, x: 40.0, y: 0.0, width: 40.0, height: 30.0)
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    resources = resolve(f, o: entry_of(pages_of(f)[0].object, key: "Resources").must())
    placed = resolve(f, o: entry_of(resources, key: "XObject").must())
    assert keys_of(placed).must() == ["Im0", "Im1", "Im2"]
    jpeg = resolve(f, o: entry_of(placed, key: "Im2").must())
    assert raw_stream_of(jpeg).must() == samples.of(samples.JPEG)
    assert stream_data_of(f, o: jpeg).is_err()
    shown = resolve(f, o: entry_of(placed, key: "Im1").must())
    assert kind_of(entry_of(shown, key: "SMask").must()) == .reference
    assert stream_data_of(f, o: shown).must().len() == 3 * 2 * 3

test "draw_table: a long table goes on to a second page with its header, read back in order"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: Size(width: 300.0, height: 200.0))
    rows: [[str]] @= [["Name", "Qty"]]

    for i in range(from: 0, to: 12)
        rows @ rows.push([f"item {i}", to_str(i * 3)])

    end = draw_table(@doc, x: 20.0, top: 180.0, bottom: 20.0, t: table([120.0, 60.0], rows: rows)).must()
    assert page_count(doc) == 2 && end > 20.0
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    second = text_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[1]).must()
    assert bytes.has_prefix(s: bytes.from_text(second), prefix: bytes.from_text("Name Qty\nitem"))

test "set_page, save_state and the pen: a footer added after every page, the state balanced"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    save_state(@doc)
    transform(@doc, matrix: rotation(10.0))
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    k @= 0

    while k < page_count(doc)
        set_page(@doc, index: k)
        draw_text(@doc, x: 150.0, y: 20.0, text: f"{k + 1} of {page_count(doc)}", align: .center).must()
        k @ k + 1

    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert text_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[1]).must() == "2 of 2"

## Examples

# The size of A4 paper, in points.
test "size_of: A4 and Letter"
    assert size_of(.letter) == Size(width: 612.0, height: 792.0)
    assert size_of(.a4).width > 595.27 && size_of(.a4).width < 595.28

# A page turned to lie on its long side.
test "to_landscape: Letter on its side"
    assert to_landscape(size_of(.letter)) == Size(width: 792.0, height: 612.0)

# A page stood upright.
test "to_portrait: a wide size made tall"
    assert to_portrait(Size(width: 300.0, height: 100.0)) == Size(width: 100.0, height: 300.0)

# An inch and a centimetre in points.
test "to_points: an inch, a pica, ten centimetres"
    assert to_points(1.0, unit: .inch) == 72.0 && to_points(1.0, unit: .pica) == 12.0
    assert to_points(2.54, unit: .cm) == 72.0

# A colour written as CSS writes it.
test "parse_color: orange in hexadecimal"
    assert parse_color("#ff8000").must() == .rgb(red: 1.0, green: 128.0 / 255.0, blue: 0.0)
    assert parse_color("#F00").must() == .rgb(red: 1.0, green: 0.0, blue: 0.0)

# The name a font has in a file.
test "base_font_of: Times bold"
    assert base_font_of(.times_bold) == "Times-Bold"

# A font found by its name in a file.
test "parse_font: Courier, and a name none has"
    assert parse_font("Courier-Oblique").must() == .courier_oblique
    assert parse_font("Arial").is_err()

# How tall Helvetica's capitals stand.
test "metrics_of: Helvetica's cap height and descent"
    m = metrics_of(.helvetica)
    assert m.cap_height == 718 && m.descent == -207 && m.box_top == 931

# A word measured as ReportLab's stringWidth measures it.
test "width_of: Hello in Helvetica at 12 points"
    w = width_of("Hello", font: .helvetica, size: 12.0).must()
    assert w > 27.3359 && w < 27.3361

# Whether a font can show a text before it is drawn.
test "has_glyphs: Latin letters in Times, Greek in Symbol"
    assert has_glyphs(.times_roman, text: "café €") && !has_glyphs(.times_roman, text: "π")
    assert has_glyphs(.symbol, text: "π")

# A document begins with no page.
test "document: no page yet"
    assert page_count(document()) == 0

# The title a viewer shows.
test "set_info: a title read back"
    doc @= document()
    set_info(@doc, key: "Title", value: "Annual report")
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a5))
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert info_of(f)["Title"].must() == "Annual report"

# A moment written as PDF writes a date.
test "format_date: the first moment of 2026"
    assert format_date(time.date(year: 2026, month: 1, day: 1).must()) == "D:20260101000000Z"

# Page content written as text, to look at it.
test "set_compression: a file whose content can be read as text"
    doc @= document()
    set_compression(@doc, on: false)
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    draw_line(@doc, from_x: 0.0, from_y: 0.0, to_x: 10.0, to_y: 10.0)
    assert bytes.contains(s: encode(doc).must(), substr: bytes.from_text("0 0 m 10 10 l S"))

# Two pages of different sizes.
test "add_page: an A4 page, then one on its side"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    add_page(@doc, size: to_landscape(size_of(.a4)))
    assert page_count(doc) == 2

# How many pages a document has.
test "page_count: three pages"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    assert page_count(doc) == 3

# Going back to the first page to draw on it again.
test "set_page: back to the first page"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_page(@doc, index: 0)
    draw_text(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 10.0, text: "first", align: .left).must()
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert text_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must() == "first"

# Text drawn in Courier.
test "set_font: Courier at 10 points"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_font(@doc, font: .courier, size: 10.0)
    draw_text(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 10.0, text: "code", align: .left).must()
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert pieces_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must()[0].font == "Courier"

# A red rectangle.
test "set_fill_color: a filled red square"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_fill_color(@doc, color: .rgb(red: 1.0, green: 0.0, blue: 0.0))
    draw_rect(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 10.0, width: 50.0, height: 50.0, paint: .fill)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A blue outline.
test "set_stroke_color: a blue circle"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_stroke_color(@doc, color: .cmyk(cyan: 1.0, magenta: 0.5, yellow: 0.0, black: 0.0))
    draw_circle(@doc, center_x: 50.0, center_y: 50.0, radius: 20.0, paint: .stroke)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A thick line.
test "set_line_width: a line three points wide"
    doc @= document()
    set_compression(@doc, on: false)
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_line_width(@doc, width: 3.0)
    assert bytes.contains(s: encode(doc).must(), substr: bytes.from_text("3 w"))

# Rounded ends.
test "set_line_cap: round ends"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_line_cap(@doc, style: .round)
    draw_line(@doc, from_x: 10.0, from_y: 10.0, to_x: 90.0, to_y: 10.0)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# Rounded corners where lines meet.
test "set_line_join: rounded corners"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_line_join(@doc, style: .round)
    draw_polyline(@doc, points: [Point(x: 0.0, y: 0.0), Point(x: 50.0, y: 50.0), Point(x: 100.0, y: 0.0)])
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# Sharp corners kept longer.
test "set_miter_limit: a limit of 4"
    doc @= document()
    set_compression(@doc, on: false)
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_miter_limit(@doc, limit: 4.0)
    assert bytes.contains(s: encode(doc).must(), substr: bytes.from_text("4 M"))

# A dashed line.
test "set_dash: dashes of 3 and gaps of 2"
    doc @= document()
    set_compression(@doc, on: false)
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_dash(@doc, lengths: [3.0, 2.0], phase: 0.0)
    assert bytes.contains(s: encode(doc).must(), substr: bytes.from_text("[3 2] 0 d"))

# A shape seen through.
test "set_opacity: a half transparent fill"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    set_opacity(@doc, fill: 0.5, stroke: 1.0)
    draw_rect(@doc, x: 0.0, y: 0.0, width: 20.0, height: 20.0, paint: .fill)
    assert bytes.contains(s: encode(doc).must(), substr: bytes.from_text("/ca 0.5 /CA 1"))

# The usual text state, changed in one field.
test "text_style: no spacing, a scale of 100"
    s = text_style()
    assert s.character_spacing == 0.0 && s.horizontal_scale == 100.0 && s.mode == .fill

# Letters spaced apart.
test "set_text_style: a point after every character"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    style @= text_style()
    style.character_spacing @ 1.0
    set_text_style(@doc, style: style)
    draw_text(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 10.0, text: "spaced", align: .left).must()
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A drawing turned, then the pen as it was.
test "save_state: a rotated drawing kept apart"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    save_state(@doc)
    transform(@doc, matrix: rotation(45.0))
    draw_rect(@doc, x: 50.0, y: 0.0, width: 20.0, height: 20.0, paint: .stroke)
    restore_state(@doc)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# The pen back as it was saved.
test "restore_state: the font as it was"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    save_state(@doc)
    set_font(@doc, font: .times_bold, size: 30.0)
    restore_state(@doc)
    draw_text(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 10.0, text: "plain", align: .left).must()
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert pieces_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must()[0].size == 12.0

# The transform that changes nothing.
test "identity: one and zeros"
    assert identity() == Matrix(a: 1.0, b: 0.0, c: 0.0, d: 1.0, e: 0.0, f: 0.0)

# A move right and up.
test "translation: ten right, five up"
    assert translation(x: 10.0, y: 5.0).e == 10.0 && translation(x: 10.0, y: 5.0).f == 5.0

# A quarter turn.
test "rotation: ninety degrees"
    m = rotation(90.0)
    assert m.b == 1.0 && m.c == -1.0

# A stretch.
test "scaling: twice as wide"
    assert scaling(x: 2.0, y: 1.0).a == 2.0

# A scale, then a move.
test "multiply: scaled twice, then moved"
    m = multiply(first: scaling(x: 2.0, y: 2.0), then: translation(x: 5.0, y: 0.0))
    assert m == Matrix(a: 2.0, b: 0.0, c: 0.0, d: 2.0, e: 5.0, f: 0.0)

# Text drawn upside down about the page's centre.
test "transform: a line of text turned half a turn"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: Size(width: 200.0, height: 200.0))
    transform(@doc, matrix: multiply(first: rotation(180.0), then: translation(x: 200.0, y: 200.0)))
    draw_text(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 10.0, text: "turned", align: .left).must()
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    piece = pieces_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must()[0]
    assert piece.x > 189.99 && piece.x < 190.01 && piece.y > 189.99

# A rule under a heading.
test "draw_line: a rule across the page"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    draw_line(@doc, from_x: 72.0, from_y: 700.0, to_x: 523.0, to_y: 700.0)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A box filled and outlined.
test "draw_rect: a filled and outlined box"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    draw_rect(@doc, x: 72.0, y: 600.0, width: 100.0, height: 50.0, paint: .fill_and_stroke)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A button's shape.
test "draw_rounded_rect: corners of 6 points"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    draw_rounded_rect(@doc, x: 72.0, y: 600.0, width: 100.0, height: 30.0, radius: 6.0, paint: .stroke)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A dot.
test "draw_circle: a filled dot"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    draw_circle(@doc, center_x: 100.0, center_y: 100.0, radius: 3.0, paint: .fill)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# An oval.
test "draw_ellipse: twice as wide as tall"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    draw_ellipse(@doc, center_x: 100.0, center_y: 100.0, radius_x: 40.0, radius_y: 20.0, paint: .stroke)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A triangle.
test "draw_polygon: a filled triangle"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    draw_polygon(@doc, points: [Point(x: 0.0, y: 0.0), Point(x: 50.0, y: 80.0), Point(x: 100.0, y: 0.0)], paint: .fill)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A chart's line.
test "draw_polyline: three points joined"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    draw_polyline(@doc, points: [Point(x: 0.0, y: 10.0), Point(x: 10.0, y: 30.0), Point(x: 20.0, y: 20.0)])
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A path begins empty.
test "empty_path: nothing in it"
    assert empty_path().steps.len() == 0

# Where a path begins.
test "move_to: the start of a path"
    p @= empty_path()
    move_to(@p, x: 1.0, y: 2.0)
    assert p.steps == ["1 2 m"]

# A straight line in a path.
test "line_to: a line from the start"
    p @= empty_path()
    move_to(@p, x: 0.0, y: 0.0)
    line_to(@p, x: 10.0, y: 0.0)
    assert p.steps == ["0 0 m", "10 0 l"]

# A curve in a path.
test "curve_to: a curve pulled up"
    p @= empty_path()
    move_to(@p, x: 0.0, y: 0.0)
    curve_to(@p, control1_x: 0.0, control1_y: 10.0, control2_x: 10.0, control2_y: 10.0, x: 10.0, y: 0.0)
    assert p.steps[1] == "0 10 10 10 10 0 c"

# A path closed back to its start.
test "close_path: a triangle closed"
    p @= empty_path()
    move_to(@p, x: 0.0, y: 0.0)
    line_to(@p, x: 10.0, y: 0.0)
    line_to(@p, x: 5.0, y: 8.0)
    close_path(@p)
    assert p.steps[p.steps.len() - 1] == "h"

# A wave drawn as a path.
test "draw_path: a curve stroked"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    p @= empty_path()
    move_to(@p, x: 10.0, y: 50.0)
    curve_to(@p, control1_x: 30.0, control1_y: 90.0, control2_x: 60.0, control2_y: 10.0, x: 90.0, y: 50.0)
    draw_path(@doc, p: p, paint: .stroke)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# Text cut to a circle.
test "clip: drawing kept inside a square"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    p @= empty_path()
    move_to(@p, x: 0.0, y: 0.0)
    line_to(@p, x: 50.0, y: 0.0)
    line_to(@p, x: 50.0, y: 50.0)
    close_path(@p)
    save_state(@doc)
    clip(@doc, p: p)
    draw_rect(@doc, x: 0.0, y: 0.0, width: 100.0, height: 100.0, paint: .fill)
    restore_state(@doc)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A title centred on a page.
test "draw_text: a centred title"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    set_font(@doc, font: .helvetica_bold, size: 20.0)
    draw_text(@doc, x: 297.6, y: 780.0, text: "Title", align: .center).must()
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert text_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must() == "Title"

# A paragraph broken into lines.
test "wrap_text: a sentence in a narrow column"
    lines = wrap_text("the quick brown fox", font: .courier, size: 10.0, width: 60.0).must()
    assert lines == ["the quick", "brown fox"]

# A paragraph drawn in a box, justified.
test "draw_text_box: a justified paragraph"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    end = draw_text_box(
        @doc,
        x: 72.0,
        top: 770.0,
        width: 200.0,
        text: "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog, and again, and once more.",
        align: .justify,
        line_height: 14.0
    ).must()
    assert end < 770.0 && end > 700.0

# A table with its usual look.
test "table: two columns, a header and a row"
    t = table([100.0, 60.0], rows: [["Item", "Price"], ["Tea", "3.50"]])
    assert t.header_rows == 1 && t.columns.len() == 2 && t.font == .helvetica

# A table drawn on a page.
test "draw_table: prices in a table"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    t @= table([100.0, 60.0], rows: [["Item", "Price"], ["Tea", "3.50"], ["Coffee", "2.80"]])
    t.columns[1].align @ .right
    end = draw_table(@doc, x: 72.0, top: 770.0, bottom: 72.0, t: t).must()
    assert end < 770.0
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert text_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must() == "Item Price\nTea 3.50\nCoffee 2.80"

# A photograph placed on a page.
test "add_jpeg: a JPEG and its size in pixels"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    id = add_jpeg(@doc, data: samples.of(samples.JPEG)).must()
    assert id.width == 8 && id.height == 6

# A PNG with transparency.
test "add_png: a PNG of 3 by 2 pixels"
    doc @= document()
    id = add_png(@doc, data: samples.of(samples.PNG)).must()
    assert id.width == 3 && id.height == 2

# An image made in memory.
test "add_image: a blank gray image"
    doc @= document()
    id = add_image(@doc, img: image.blank(width: 4, height: 2, format: .gray).must()).must()
    assert id.width == 4 && id.index == 0

# An image drawn twice, written once.
test "draw_image: one image at two sizes"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    id = add_png(@doc, data: samples.of(samples.PNG)).must()
    draw_image(@doc, id: id, x: 10.0, y: 10.0, width: 30.0, height: 20.0)
    draw_image(@doc, id: id, x: 50.0, y: 10.0, width: 60.0, height: 40.0)
    assert page_count(doc) == 1

# A link to a web page.
test "add_link: a rectangle that opens an address"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    add_link(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 10.0, width: 80.0, height: 12.0, url: "https://example.org/")
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert links_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must()[0].target == .url(address: "https://example.org/")

# A link to another page of the document.
test "add_page_link: a link to the second page"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    add_page_link(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 10.0, width: 80.0, height: 12.0, page: 1, top: 400.0)
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert links_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must()[0].target == .page(index: 1, top: 400.0)

# An outline of two chapters, the second with a section.
test "add_bookmark: chapters and a section"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    add_bookmark(@doc, title: "Chapter 1", level: 0, top: 400.0)
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a6))
    add_bookmark(@doc, title: "Chapter 2", level: 0, top: 400.0)
    add_bookmark(@doc, title: "Section 2.1", level: 1, top: 200.0)
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    marks = outline_of(f).must()
    assert marks.len() == 3 && marks[2].title == "Section 2.1" && marks[2].level == 1 && marks[2].target == .page(index: 1, top: 200.0)

# The bytes of a file, ready to be written.
test "encode: a file that begins with its header"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    data = encode(doc).must()
    assert bytes.has_prefix(s: data, prefix: bytes.from_text("%PDF-1.7"))

# The usual limits.
test "default_limits: a million objects"
    assert default_limits().objects == 1000000 && default_limits().depth == 256

# A file read from its bytes.
test "decode: ReportLab's one page"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert pages_of(f).len() == 1

# The version a file says it follows.
test "version_of: ReportLab's 1.3"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert version_of(f) == "1.3"

# Whether a file was encrypted.
test "is_encrypted: RC4, opened"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.RC4_128), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert is_encrypted(f)

# The pages of a file, with their sizes.
test "pages_of: the size of the first page"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert pages_of(f)[0].media_box == Box(left: 0.0, bottom: 0.0, right: 595.2756, top: 841.8898)

# The text of a page, as pdftotext reads it.
test "text_of: two lines of ReportLab's"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert text_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must() == "Hello from reportlab\nSecond line, café €5"

# Where each run of text stands.
test "pieces_of: the first run, where it begins"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    first = pieces_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must()[0]
    assert first.text == "Hello from reportlab" && first.x == 72.0 && first.y == 700.0 && first.size == 14.0 && first.font == "Helvetica"

# The title and author of a file.
test "info_of: ReportLab's title and author"
    info = info_of(decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must())
    assert info["Title"].must() == "Small title" && info["Author"].must() == "Ana"

# A date with an offset, as an instant.
test "parse_date: noon an hour east of Greenwich"
    t = parse_date("D:20240301120000+01'00'").must()
    assert time.unix_seconds(t) == 1709290800

# The outline of a file.
test "outline_of: pypdf's three bookmarks"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.OUTLINE), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert outline_of(f).must()[0].title == "First"

# The links of a page.
test "links_of: a link to an address"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.OUTLINE), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert links_of(f, page: pages_of(f)[0]).must().len() == 1

# A title stored as UTF-16.
test "decode_text: UTF-16BE after its mark"
    assert decode_text([0xfe, 0xff, 0x03, 0xa9]) == "Ω"

# Text made into a PDF string.
test "encode_text: Latin-1 stays one byte, Greek needs UTF-16"
    assert encode_text("é") == [0xe9]
    assert encode_text("Ω") == [0xfe, 0xff, 0x03, 0xa9]

# An object by its number.
test "object_of: the catalogue's object"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    root = reference_of(entry_of(trailer_of(f), key: "Root").must()).must()
    assert name_of(entry_of(object_of(f, number: root.number), key: "Type").must()).must() == "Catalog"

# A reference followed.
test "resolve: the trailer's Root"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert kind_of(resolve(f, o: entry_of(trailer_of(f), key: "Root").must())) == .dictionary

# The trailer of a file.
test "trailer_of: its Size"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert int_of(entry_of(trailer_of(f), key: "Size").must()).must() > 5

# The catalogue of a file.
test "catalog_of: its page tree"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert kind_of(entry_of(catalog_of(f), key: "Pages").must()) == .reference

# What kind an object is.
test "kind_of: a page's MediaBox is an array"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert kind_of(entry_of(pages_of(f)[0].object, key: "MediaBox").must()) == .array

# A boolean of a file.
test "bool_of: a boolean, and a name that is none"
    assert !bool_of(Object(state: .boolean(value: true))).is_err()
    assert bool_of(Object(state: .name(value: "x"))).is_err()

# An integer of a file.
test "int_of: the page count"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert int_of(entry_of(resolve(f, o: entry_of(catalog_of(f), key: "Pages").must()), key: "Count").must()).must() == 1

# A number, whole or not.
test "number_of: the width of an A4 page"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    box = items_of(entry_of(pages_of(f)[0].object, key: "MediaBox").must()).must()
    assert number_of(box[2]).must() > 595.27

# The bytes of a string.
test "bytes_of: the producer's bytes"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    info = resolve(f, o: entry_of(trailer_of(f), key: "Info").must())
    assert decode_text(bytes_of(entry_of(info, key: "Author").must()).must()) == "Ana"

# A name's text.
test "name_of: a page's type"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert name_of(entry_of(pages_of(f)[0].object, key: "Type").must()).must() == "Page"

# The items of an array.
test "items_of: the four numbers of a box"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert items_of(entry_of(pages_of(f)[0].object, key: "MediaBox").must()).must().len() == 4

# An entry of a dictionary.
test "entry_of: a key held and one absent"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()

    match entry_of(catalog_of(f), key: "Absent")
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND

# The keys of a dictionary.
test "keys_of: the catalogue's keys"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    names = keys_of(catalog_of(f)).must()
    assert names == ["PageMode", "Pages", "Type"]

# A reference's number.
test "reference_of: the trailer's Root"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    assert reference_of(entry_of(trailer_of(f), key: "Root").must()).must().number > 0

# A page's content, decompressed.
test "stream_data_of: the operators of a page"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    content = resolve(f, o: entry_of(pages_of(f)[0].object, key: "Contents").must())
    assert bytes.contains(s: stream_data_of(f, o: content).must(), substr: bytes.from_text("Tj"))

# A page's content as the file holds it.
test "raw_stream_of: compressed bytes"
    f = decode(samples.of(samples.SMALL), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    content = resolve(f, o: entry_of(pages_of(f)[0].object, key: "Contents").must())
    assert raw_stream_of(content).must().len() > 0

## Embedded fonts

# The sample font as a face made by `load_font`.
function noto() -> Font
    return load_font(font_samples.noto_sans(), index: 0).must()

# The measures of the sample's font as fontTools reads them, and the width of a
# text from its `hmtx`: A is 639 and H 741 in the 1000 units of the em.
test "load_font: the name, the metrics, the width of a text and the glyphs it has"
    f = noto()
    assert base_font_of(f) == "NotoSans-Regular"
    m = metrics_of(f)
    assert m.ascent == 1069 && m.descent == -293 && m.cap_height == 714 && m.x_height == 536
    assert m.box_left == -621 && m.box_top == 1067 && m.underline_position == -100
    w = width_of("AH", font: f, size: 10.0).must()
    assert w > 13.7999 && w < 13.8001
    assert has_glyphs(f, text: "Zażółć gęślą jaźń; Čeština, kůň, Řeřicha, žluť; Αλφα Ω €")
    assert !has_glyphs(f, text: "日本") && !has_glyphs(f, text: "é")
    assert width_of("", font: f, size: 10.0).must() == 0.0

# A font that is no font, a font with CFF outlines, a licence that bars it.
test "load_font: no font, CFF outlines and a restricted licence fail"
    assert load_font([0, 1, 2, 3], index: 0).is_err()
    assert load_font(font_samples.cff(), index: 0).is_err()
    assert load_font(font_samples.with_licence(2), index: 0).is_err()

    match load_font(font_samples.with_licence(0x100), index: 0)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "unsupported"

# A character the font lacks fails `unsupported`, names the font and the character, and draws nothing.
test "draw_text: a character the embedded font has no glyph for draws nothing"
    doc @= document()
    set_compression(@doc, on: false)
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    set_font(@doc, font: noto(), size: 12.0)

    match draw_text(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 20.0, text: "A日", align: .left)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "unsupported" && e.msg == "NotoSans-Regular has no glyph for \"日\" (U+65E5)"

    assert !bytes.contains(s: encode(doc).must(), substr: bytes.from_text("/Subtype /Type0"))

# The code of a glyph is its number: A is 34 and H 41, two bytes each.
test "draw_text: the glyphs of an embedded font are written as two-byte codes with TJ"
    doc @= document()
    set_compression(@doc, on: false)
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    set_font(@doc, font: noto(), size: 12.0)
    draw_text(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 20.0, text: "AH", align: .left).must()
    data = encode(doc).must()
    assert bytes.contains(s: data, substr: bytes.from_text("BT /F14 12 Tf 10 20 Td [<00220029>] TJ ET"))
    assert bytes.contains(s: data, substr: bytes.from_text("/Resources 3 0 R"))
    assert bytes.contains(s: data, substr: bytes.from_text("/Font << /F14 "))

# Tw reaches one-byte codes alone, so a word's spacing is an adjustment after each space glyph.
test "draw_text: word spacing is a TJ adjustment after each space, not Tw"
    doc @= document()
    set_compression(@doc, on: false)
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    set_font(@doc, font: noto(), size: 12.0)
    style @= text_style()
    style.word_spacing @ 2.0
    set_text_style(@doc, style: style)
    draw_text(@doc, x: 10.0, y: 20.0, text: "A H", align: .left).must()
    data = encode(doc).must()
    assert bytes.contains(
        s: data,
        substr: bytes.from_text("BT /F14 12 Tf 10 20 Td [<00220001> -166.6667 <0029>] TJ ET")
    )
    w = width_of("A H", font: noto(), size: 12.0).must()
    assert w > 19.6799 && w < 19.6801

# Written and read back: the text, in Polish, Czech and Greek, through the ToUnicode map.
test "encode and decode: Polish, Czech and Greek lines are read back as they were drawn"
    doc @= document()
    add_page(@doc, size: size_of(.a4))
    set_font(@doc, font: noto(), size: 14.0)
    draw_text(@doc, x: 72.0, y: 700.0, text: "Zażółć gęślą jaźń", align: .left).must()
    draw_text(@doc, x: 72.0, y: 680.0, text: "Čeština, kůň, Řeřicha, žluť", align: .left).must()
    draw_text(@doc, x: 72.0, y: 660.0, text: "Αλφα Βητα Γαμμα Δελτα Ω €5", align: .left).must()
    f = decode(encode(doc).must(), password: "", limits: default_limits()).must()
    page = pages_of(f)[0]
    assert text_of(f, page: page).must() == "Zażółć gęślą jaźń\nČeština, kůň, Řeřicha, žluť\nΑλφα Βητα Γαμμα Δελτα Ω €5"
    pieces = pieces_of(f, page: page).must()
    assert pieces.len() == 3 && pieces[0].size == 14.0 && pieces[0].font == "NotoSans-Regular"
